ªksfy;e vkSj izkÑfrd xSl ea=h \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Oil and Natural Gas Corporation Limited makes payment of royalty on post -\ndiscount prices to the States;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n \n(c) whether the royalty being given to the States is based on pre-discounted prices of crude oil \nand if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkÑfrd xSl ea=h (Lora= izHkkj) \n¼Jh /keZsUnz iz/kku½ \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (I/C) IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN)  \n \n \n(a) to (c):     At present there is no discount on sale of crude oil by Oil and Natural Gas \nCorporation Limited (ONGC) and royalty is being paid to States on the basis of price realized \nby ONGC.  \n \nx-x-x-x-x \n  \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |74", "t": "Nuclear Power Generation", "head": "1\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n74\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n21.11.2016\n \nKARTIKA 30\n, 193\n8\n \n(SAKA)\n \n \n \nMAINTENANCE OF HERITAGE SITES BY PRIVATE SECTOR \n \n \n \n*74.\n \nSHRI  R. GOPALAKRISHNAN:\n \nSHRI  HARISH CHANDER \nALIAS\n  \nHARISH DWIVEDI: \n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nthe number of historical monuments and heritage sites maintained by the \nArchaeological Survey of India (ASI) State/UT\n-\nwise; \n \n(b)\n \nwhether heritage sites in the country are being given to private compa\nnies for \ntheir renovation and maintenance and if so, the details thereof and the reasons \ntherefor; \n \n(c)\n \nthe other steps taken by the Government to promote private sector’s \ninvolvement in heritage promotion along with the details thereof; and \n \n(d)\n \nwhether\n \nASI has undertaken responsibility of maintaining historical/heritage \nsites situated in foreign countries also and if so, the details thereof?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE, CULTURE AND TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)\n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n(a)to(d)\n \nA Stateme\nnt is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n\n2\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (\nd\n) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n74\n \nFOR \n21.11.2016\n \n \n \n(a)\n \nThe State\n-\nwise list of 3686 monuments/sites \nmaintained by the \nArchaeological Survey of India in the countr\ny \nis at Annexure. \n \n \n(b)\n \nAs on date, Archaeological Survey of India has undertaken conservation \nand development of Humayun’s Tomb, Delhi in collaboration with Aga \nKhan Trust for Culture (AKTC) and conservation and development of \nTomb of Mohammed Shah known\n \nas Mubarak Shah’s Gumbad, Delhi in \ncollaboration with Indian National Trust for Cultural Heritage (INTACH). \nFurther MOU’s have been signed with State Governments for \nmaintenance and upgrading visitor facilities.  However, in no case whole \nwork is entruste\nd to any private company. \n \n(c)\n \nArchaeological Survey of India has issued an Expression of Interest on \nPublic Private Partnership Module (PPP) for providing World class \nfacilities at the important tourist visited monuments/sites.  Under this \nExpression of \nInterest (EOI) 20 different services which includes web\n-\nbased information and other innovative ideas which enhance the \npopularity of the monument and bring it at par have been offered.  \n \n(d)\n \nOn request from MEA the ASI has taken up conservation work of Ta\n \nProhm Temple in Combodia, Vat Phom temple in Lao PDR and Anande \nTemple in Myanmar.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n3\n \n \nANNEXURE \n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 74 FOR 21.11.2016\n \n \nNUMBER OF CENTRALLY PROTECTED MONUMENTS/SITES UNDE\nR THE JURISDICTION \nOF ARCHAEOLOGICAL SURVEY OF INDIA IN THE COUNTRY \n \n \n \nSl.No.\n \nName of State\n \nNos. of Monuments\n \n1\n.\n \n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n129\n \n2\n.\n \n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n03\n \n3\n.\n \n \nAssam\n \n55\n \n4\n.\n \n \nBihar\n \n70\n \n5\n.\n \n \nChhattisgarh\n \n47\n \n6\n.\n \n \nDaman & Diu (U. T.)\n \n12\n \n7\n.\n \n \nGoa\n \n21\n \n8\n.\n \n \nGujarat\n \n203\n \n9\n.\n \n \nHaryana\n \n91\n \n10\n.\n \n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n40\n \n11\n.\n \n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n69\n \n12\n.\n \n \nJharkhand\n \n13\n \n13\n.\n \n \nKarnataka \n \n506\n \n14\n.\n \n \nKerala\n \n28\n \n15\n.\n \n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n292\n \n16\n.\n \n \nMaharashtra\n \n285\n \n17\n.\n \n \nManipur\n \n01\n \n18\n.\n \n \nMeghalaya\n \n08\n \n19\n.\n \n \nMizoram\n \n01\n \n20\n.\n \n \nNagaland\n \n04\n \n21\n.\n \n \nN.C.T. Delhi\n \n174\n \n22\n.\n \n \nOdisha\n \n79\n \n23\n.\n \n \nPuducherry (U.T.)\n \n07\n \n24\n.\n \n \nPunjab\n \n33\n \n25\n.\n \n \nRajastha\nn\n \n162\n \n26\n.\n \n \nSikkim\n \n03\n \n27\n.\n \n \nTelangana\n \n08\n \n28\n.\n \n \nTamil Nadu\n \n413\n \n29\n.\n \n \nTripura\n \n08\n \n30\n.\n \n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n743\n \n31\n.\n \n \nUttarakhand\n \n42\n \n32\n.\n \n \nWest Bengal\n \n \n136\n \n \nTOTAL\n \n3686\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |740", "t": "Free Education for Martyrs' Children", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT  \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY  \n \nLOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 740 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st NOVEMBER, 2016 \n \nFree Education for Martyrs' Children \n \n740. SHRIMATI VASANTHI M.: \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government proposes to provide free education to martyrs' children in all the \nschools in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government has consulted the families of martyrs' throughout the country and \ntaken any survey about this, if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(c) if not, the time by which the final decision is likely to be taken in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n (SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n \n(a) to (c):  The Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Edu cation (RTE) Act, 2009, is \neffective from 1st April 2010, confers a right on every child of the age of six to fourteen years to \nfree and compulsory education in a nei ghbourhood school till completion of elementary \neducation. Accordingly, elementary education is  free in all government schools and further all \ngovernment aided schools have to provide fr ee education to such proportion of children \nadmitted, as in the proportion of the government  grants received to their annual recurring \nexpenses, subject to a minimu m of 25%. Further, all privat e un-aided schools shall admit \nchildren belonging to weaker section and disadvantaged gr oup, as notified by appropriate \nGovernment, in the neighbourhood in class I (or pre-sc hool, as the case may be), to the extent of \nat least 25 percent of the stre ngth of that class and provide free and compulsory elementary \neducation to them till its completion. In view of this, there is no proposal in this Department, at \npresent, regarding a separate provision for the children of martyrs. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |741", "t": "Employability of Vocational Courses", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 741 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nEmployability of Vocational Courses \n 741. SHRI A.T. NANA PATIL: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has chalked out an y format for bringing equivalence in the \nformal and vocational streams of education in the country ; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(d) the fresh steps taken/proposed to be  taken by the Government to provide \nopportunity to the students of vocational courses to move up in the education ladder \nby pursuing higher education and improving their chances to get better jobs? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(DR MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n***** \n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam. A framework of Skills Assessment Matrix for Vocational \nAdvancement of Youth (SAMVAY) envisages for horizontal and vertical movement \nbetween the skills education and the formal education has been issued. The framework \nprovides equivalence between 10 th/ 12 th classes in formal system with the skill-based \neducation.  \n \n(c)  : Doesn’t arise. \n \n(d)  : Apart from the above, the following schemes are being implemented for \nimparting employability skills in the education and opportunity to pursue higher \neducation:  \n (i) Community College scheme  \n (ii)  Bachelor in Vocational (B Voc) Programme \n(iii) Deen Dayal Upadhyay Kaushal Kendras in higher educational  \ninstitutions. \n (iv) Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yoja na (PMKVY) by Technical Institutes.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |742", "t": "2nd Generation Reforms ESIC - 2.0", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n LOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 742 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \n2ND GENERATION REFORMS ESIC - 2.0 \n \n742. SHRI S.P. MUDDAHANUME GOWDA: \n        KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether Employees' State Insurance Corporation (ESIC) has    \nlaunched 2 nd Generation Reforms ESIC -2.0 and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(b)whether ESIC also proposes to increase the coverage  aspects/ \nsphere of ESI scheme so that more and more workers of \n     the country get the social security benefits including medical care; \nand if so, the details thereof; \n(c)whether ESIC proposes to bear the expenses on super speciality \ntreatment of ESIC be neficiaries over and above the expenditure by \nState Governments, if so, the details thereof; and \n(d)whether the decision related to preexisting diseases not to be \nconsidered for super speciality treatment will only be with \nprospective effect and if so, the details thereof? \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n(a)- The second generation reforms i.e. ESIC 2.0 was launched by the \nHon'ble Prime Minister on 20.07.2015. Three broad group  of agendas \nnamely- extending the coverage of ESI Scheme, reforms in health \nservices and Improving patients/ attendant care were unveiled. Details \nare as below:- \nContd..2/- \n \n\n::  2  :: \nAgenda-1- Expansion- \n1. Extending the coverage of ESI Scheme in all States/UTs. \n2. Covering whole of 393 districts where the scheme is presently \npartially implemented in Centres. \n3. To cover all the construction workers under ESIC. \n4. To open health scheme for selected group, of unorganised workers \nlike rickshaw -pullers/auto-rickshaw drivers in selected \nurban/metropolitan areas. \n5. To start setting up State ESI Corporation/societies in all states as \nsubsidiary of ESI Corporation to facilitate quicker disbursal of benefit \nand improvement in health facilities, decentralized decision making \nsystem are to be put in place. \nAgenda – 2: Reforms in Health Services- \n1. Computerization of its core activities and its records. The health \nrecords of the Insured Persons to be preserved in electronic form. \n2. All dispensaries, in a phased  manner, to be converted to six bedded \nhospitals. \n3. Daily change of bed-sheets in hospitals on different colour. \n4. 24X7 Medical Helpline \n5. Evening OPD for senior citizens and Differently – abled Patients. \n6.To start appropriate cancer/cardiology detecti on/ treatment \nfacilitates at different level of hospitals. \n7. To provide all possible pathological facilities in all hospitals \npremises. \n8.To provide dialysis facilities  in all ESIC Model Hospitals on PPP \nModel. \nContd…3/-  \n\n::  3  :: \nAgenda – 3 : Improving patients/attendant Care- \n1) To put in place appropriate Queue Management System  \n2) To provide behavioral training to para -medical and other staff of the \nhospitals \n3) To provide for a feedback system, for all Indoor patients.  \n4) To create at least one Mo ther and Child Care Hospital with higher \nfacilities  in every state. \n5) To provide for facility of yoga on all our hospitals. \n6) To create tele-medecine facilities for the beneficiaries in phases. \n7) AYUSH facilities to be extended up to the dispensary lev el in \nphases, the first phase. \n \n(b):  The Employees State Insurance Corporation(ESIC) has approved \nthe enhancement of wage ceiling from present Rs. 15,000 per month to \nRs. 21,000/-. The draft Rules calling for objections has been published \nin Gazette of In dia on 06.10.2016. This enhancement of wage ceiling \nshall bring more employees under ESIC coverage. In addition, the \ndecision has also been taken to ensure coverage of the Scheme in all \ndistricts of the Country. \n(c):  Yes, Madam. The ESIC in its meeting da ted 07/08/2015 has \ndecided to bear the expenses on super specialty treatment over and \nabove the expenditure of state government. \n(d):    Yes, Madam. The ESIC in its 166th Corporation meetings held on \n07.08.2015 has decided to consider eligibility of pre existing diseases \ni.e. for malignancy & dialysis as prospective w.e.f. 30.08.2016.  \n         Further, ESIC has revised eligibility for Super Specialty including \nthe children of Insured Persons with congenital diseases & genetic \ndisorders. \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |743", "t": "Uchchtar Avishkar Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 743 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nUchchtar Avishkar Yojana \n \n743.  SHRI DUSHYANT CHAUTALA: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has introduced Uchchtar Avishkar Yojana towards \ninnovative ideas in education in the country;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the financial allocation made by the \nGovernment for this purpose; and \n \n(c)  the total number of innovative ideas received by the Government under this \nprogramme at present? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n \n(a) & (b) : Yes, Madam.  With a view to  promoting research and innovation of a \nhigher order that directly impacts the needs of the Industry and thereby improves \nthe competitive edge of Indian manufacturing, Government launched the Scheme \nUchhatar Avishkar Yojana (UAY) in 2015 with financial outlay of Rs. 475.00 crore \nfor a period of two years beginning from 2016-17.  The project cost under UAY is \nmet to the extent of 25% by the Industry , 50% by the Ministry of Human Resource \nDevelopment and the remaining 25% by the concerned Ministry/Department.  \nDuring 2016-17, an amount of Rs.75.00 crore has been allocated to the Scheme, \nout of which Rs.37.50 crore has been rele ased for implementation of the approved \nprojects.  \n \n(c)     : Government received (180) project proposals, out of which (92) projects have \nbeen approved by the Apex Committee of UAY.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |744", "t": "Medical Degree Colleges", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n  \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.744 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST NOVEMBER, 2016 \n \nMedical Degree Colleges \n \n†744. SHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to establish any institute in the field of \neducation in the State of Jharkhand; \n(b) ) if so, the time by which it is likely to be done and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n(c) the steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n(a) to (c): Ministry of Health & Family Welfare has informed that they are administering a \nCentrally Sponsored Scheme for “Establishment of New Medical Colleges attached with \ndistrict/ referral Hospitals”. Under the Scheme, three District Hospitals at Dumka, \nHazaribag and Palamu (Daltonganj) in Jharkhand State have been approved for \nupgradation to new Medical Colleges and Rs.48 crore has been released as Central \nShare till date. \nMinistry of Human Resource Development is administering a Centrally Sponsored \nScheme,RashtriyaUchchatarShikshaAbhiyan (RUSA) under whichcentral assistance for \nupgradation of Ranchi college to University and creation of one new Professional \n(Technical) College in East Singhbhum District has been approved by the RUSA Project \nApproval Board (PAB). 1\nst instalment of Central share have been sanctioned to the State \nto the tune of Rs. 16.5 crore for upgradation of Ranchi college and Rs. 7.8 crore for \ncreation of 1 new professional (technical) college in East Singhbhum district. \nThe Ministry has also established Indian In stitute of Informati on Technology (IIIT), \nRanchi under the Scheme “Setting up of 20 IIITs in Not for Profit Public Private \nPartnership (IIIT-N-PPP) Mode” .  Approval for setting up this IIIT at Ranchi, was \nconveyed on 22.4.2015. An amount of Rs. 97,62,500/- has been released to IIIT Ranchi \nduring 2015-16.  The IIIT Ranchi has started academic session in 2016-2017 with 52 \nstudents. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |745", "t": "Field of Education", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 745 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nField of Education \n \n745.  SHRI BADRUDDIN AJMAL:  \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has signed bilateral agreements with various \ncountries to strengthen international cooperation in the field of education \nduring the last three years and the current year; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the name of the countries; and \n \n(c) the progress achieved so far in this regard? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n(a) & (b): A list of countries with which bilateral agreements have been signed by \nMinistry of Human Resource Development during the last three years on cooperation \nin the field of higher education is annexed. In the current year, no bilateral \nagreement has so far been signed at Government level.  \n \n(c)  The main objective of bilateral agreements includes exchange of students/ \nfaculty/experts, sharing of informatio n relating to education, holding of \nconferences/seminars, Joint Research Programmes and research collaboration \namongst higher educational institutions. Implementation of these activities is a \ncontinuous process in which the implementing agencies in India carry out activities in \nconsultation with agencies in partner countries, as per the terms of the MoU.  \n*** \n  \n\nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a&b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 745 FOR 21.11.2016 ASKED BY HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT SHRI \nBADRUDDIN AJMAL REGARDING FIELD OF EDUCATION \n \nLIST OF COUNTRIES WITH WHICH GOVERNMEN T OF INDIA HAS SIGNED MEMORANDUM OF \nUNDERSTANDING (MOU)/EDUCATIONAL EXCHANGE PROGRAMME (EEP)/JOINT STATEMENT/ \nJOINT DECLARATION OF INTENT (JDI) FOR COOPERATION IN EDUCATION, DURING THE \nLAST THREE YEARS \n \nS. NO.  NAME OF THE COUNTRY DATE OF SIGNING \n1 GERMANY 11.04.2013 (MOU) \n05.10.2015(JDI) \n2 PERU 28.10.2013 (EEP) \n3 ESTONIA 15.10.2013 (EEP) \n4 KUWAIT 08.11.2013 (EEP) \n5 HUNGARY 19.11.2014 (EEP) \n6 UNITED KINGDOM 13.11.2014(Joint Statement) \n18.03.2015/07.04.2015 (UKIERI *- MOU) \n7 UNITED STATES OF AMERICA 23.01.2015 (JDI) \n8 CHINA 15.05.2015 (EEP) \n9 AUSTRALIA 24.08.2015 (MOU) \n10 UNITED ARAB EMIRATES 03.09.2015 (MOU) \n11 JAPAN 11.12.2015 (MOU) \n  \n*UKIERI - United Kingdom-India Education and Research Initiative "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |746", "t": "Educational Institutes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 746 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nEducational Institutes \n \n746. SHRI SUNIL KUMAR MONDAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Educational inst itutions are being set up on th e lines of Industries in the \ncountry and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether the fund has been allotte d for this purpose and if so, the details thereof and if \nnot, the the reasons therefor; and  \n(c) the steps taken by the Government and the time by which the programme is likely to be \ncompleted?  \n  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE \nDEVELOPMENT \n                             (DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n(a) : No Madam. \n(b) & (c) :   Do not arise. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |747", "t": "Yatri Niwas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†747 \nANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nYATRI NIWAS \n \n†747. SHRI ASHOK MAHADEORAO NETE:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the total number of 'Yatri Niwas' in various Sta tes/UTs at \npresent, location-wise;  \n(b)  whether there is any proposal to construct new 'Yatri Niwas' \nin various States during the current financial year; and  \n(c)  if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPEN DENT CHARGE) \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a) to (c): Development of accommodation infrastructure is \nprimarily the responsibility of the concerned State Government/UT \nAdministration. \n \n The scheme of the Ministry of Tourism for providing financial \nassistance for co nstruction of Yatri Niwas has been discontinued \nfrom the 10th Plan onwards.  \n \n The State -wise and location -wise details of Yatri Niwas \nsanctioned by the Ministry of Tourism during the 8th and 9th Plan \nare at Annexure. \n \n********\n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART S (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†747 ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \nREGARDING YATRI NIWAS. \n \nProjects sanctioned by Ministry of Tourism for Yatri Niwas during 8 th \nand 9th Plans. \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the \nState/UT \nNo. of \nProjects \nLocation \n1. Andaman & \nNicobar \n1 (i) Karmatang \n2. Andhra Pradesh 7 (i) Arraku Valley \n(ii) Vishakapatnam \n(iii) Tirupati \n(iv) Yodagirigutta \n(v) Horsely Hills \n(vi) Tirumala-Tirupathi \n(vii) Nagarjuna Sagar \n3. Arunachal \nPradesh \n2 (i) Bameng \n(ii) Roing (Dibang) Valley  \n4. Assam 3 (i) Barpeta \n(ii) Bhuban Hills, Silchar \n(iii) Batadrawa \n5. Himachal \nPradesh \n4 (i) Deotridh \n(ii) Jawalamukhi \n(iii) Bharmour \n(iv) Dharamshala \n6. Jammu & \nKashmir \n3 (i) Vaishno Devi \n(ii) Baba Rishi \n(iii) Patnitop \n7. Karnataka 10 (i) Dharwar  \n(ii) Gulbarga \n(iii) Bijapur  \n(iv) Maravanthe \n(v) Udipi \n(vi) Hampi \n(vii) Hassan \n(viii) Yellammanagudda  \n(ix) Sravanabelagola \n(x) Talakad, Mysore \n\n8. Kerala 6 (i) Peerumedu  \n(ii) Calicut \n(iii) Malayattur \n(iv) Alleppey \n(v) Kondotti \n(vi) Kumily \n9. Madhya \nPradesh \n1 (i) Hossangabad \n10. Maharashtra 1 (i) Jothiba, Kolhapur \n11. Manipur 1 (i) Imphal \n12. Orissa 4 (i) Puri  \n(ii) Chandipur \n(iii) Satpuda (Chilka Lake) \n(iv) Konark \n13. Punjab 1 (i) Fatehgarh Sahib \n14. Tamil Nadu 3 (i) Rameshwaram \n(ii) Samayapuram \n(iii) Tiruvananmalai \n15. Uttarakhand 2 (i) Gauchar \n(ii) Guptkashi (Rudraprayag) \n16. West Bengal 1 (i) New Jalpaiguri \n                                            \n********** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |748", "t": "National Cultural Fund and PPP Model", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNTARRED QUESTION NO.\n748\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11\n.2016\n \n \nNational Culture Fund and PPP Model\n \n \n748\n. \n  \nSHRI \nC. MAHENDRAN\n:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to\n \nstate:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nWhether the Government is promotin\ng Public Private Partnership in \nthe   country in the field of culture\n \nand if so, the details thereof\n;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nthe list of such projects, State\n-\nwise;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether the National Culture Fund  wa\ns established by the \nGovernment\n;\n \na\nnd if so, the details thereof\n; and\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nthe key r\neasons behind such an establishment?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF \nSTATE (IC) FOR CULTURE & TOURISM\n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA\n)\n \n \n \n(a) to (d)  Yes Madam. Recognizing the need to introduce innovative patterns \nof cultural funding, the Government of India, through the Minis\ntry of Culture \n(the then Department of Culture), set up the National Culture Fund (NCF) as \na trust, under the Charitable Endowments Act 1890, in the year 1996 with a \nview to augment its efforts and to facilitate public\n-\nprivate partnership in the \nfield of h\neritage conservation and promotion. The conservation and \nrestoration of Central/State protected monuments are done in collaboration \nwith the Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) and the state archaeological \ndepartments.\n \n \nNCF already has such partnerships w\nith some Public Sector Undertakings like \nthe National Thermal Power Corporation Ltd. (NTPC), Oil and Natural Gas \nCorporation Ltd. (ONGC), Steel Authority of India Ltd. (SAIL), Housing and \nUrban Development Corporation Ltd. (HUDCO), Rural Electrification \nCo\nrporation Ltd. (REC) and with Public and Private Sector Enterprises like \nIndia Infrastructure Finance Company Ltd. (IIFCL), State Bank of India (SBI) \nand UCO Bank, Sony India Pvt. Ltd. etc. towards its efforts on preservation \nand conservation of our cultur\nal heritage. The lists of such projects are \nattached as \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nand \nAnnexure\n-\nII\n.  \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE\n–\nI\n \n \nAnnexure referred in part (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha unstarred Question No.748 for Reply \non 21.11.2016 regarding National Culture Fund and PPP Model \n \n \nONGOING\n \nPROJECTS\n \nS.No\n \nProject\n \nSponsors\n \nDate/Year of\n \nMOU Signed\n \n \nNew Delhi\n \n1.\n \nJantar Mantar, \nNewDelhi\n \nRs.10 lakhs funds were enhanced to \nRs.16 lakhs \n \nM/s Apeejay Surrendra Park \nHotels Ltd. \n–\n \nNCF\n-\nASI \n \n \nRs. 16 lakhs\n \n \n11.10.2000\n \n2.\n \nConservation and preservation of\n \nLodhi Tombs \n \nSteel Authority of India\n \nRs. 50 lakhs\n \n10.01.2006\n \n3.\n \nConservation & maintenance of \nTughlaqabad Fort\n \nGAIL India Ltd.\n \nRs.30 lakhs\n \n \n13.04.2009\n \n4.\n \nConservation of Jaiprakash Yantra, \nJantarMantar\n \nState Trading Corporation\n \n \nRs.32 lakhs\n \n28.11.2013\n \n5.\n \nUpgradation of ASI Site museums \nSwatantrata Sangram \nSangrahalaya, Red Fort\n \nBHEL\n \nRs. 2 crores\n \n \n \n30.10.2014\n \n \nKARNATAKA\n \n \n \n6.\n \nKrishna Temple, Hampi\n \nHampi Foundation & WMF \n \nRs.4 Crores\n \n12.06.2008\n \n7.\n \nGardens of Ibrahim Rauza and \nGolGumbaz, Bijapur\n \nNauras \nTrust \n \n \nRs.30 lakhs\n \n \n11.12.2009\n \n \nRAJASTHAN\n \n \n8.\n \nConservation and Restoration of \nJaisalmer Fort\n \nWorld Monument Fund\n \n(Rs.1.96crores)ASI (Rs.4Cr)  \n \nTotal \n-\n \nRs. 5.96Cr \n \n13.08.2003\n \n9.\n \nPreparation of DPR for Old \nRangnathMandir, Pushkar\n \n(NCF\n-\nTemple trust)\n \nTempl\ne Trust \n–\n \nRs.5 lakhs + \ntaxes\n \nNCF Rs.3.50 lakhs \n \nTotal \n–\n \nRs.8.5 lakhs\n \n \n21.7.2011\n \n \nWEST BENGAL \n \n10.\n \nAlambazar Math Project, Kolkata\n \nNon ASI project\n \n \nNCF \n–\n \nRs.1.0