espectively.  \n \nThe State/UT -wise break -up of DTVs and FTVs, including the \nStates of Jammu & Kashmir and North Eastern States, during 2014 \nand 2015 are given in Annexure.  \n \n(c) and (d):  Development and promotion of tourism sector is the \nresponsibility of the State Governments and Union Territory \nAdministrations. The Ministry of Touri sm provides Central Financial \nAssistance (CFA), from various plan schemes, to them on receipt of \nproposals for tourism projects subject to adherence of existing \n\nscheme guidelines, availability of funds and liquidation of utilisation \ncertificates for funds released earlier. \n \nIn addition, Government has undertaken the following special \nmeasures for development and promotion of tourism in Jammu & \nKashmir and North East Region:  \n \ni. Provision of complimentary space to the State of Jammu \n& Kashmir and North East ern States in India Pavilions \nset up at major International Travel Fairs and \nExhibitions. \nii. Government of India employees are allowed to convert \ntheir home town LTC to all India LTC for visiting Jammu \n& Kashmir and North East Region.  \niii. 100% central fi nancial assistance for organizing fairs & \nfestivals is allowed to the North Eastern States and the \nState of Jammu & Kashmir.  Ministry of Tourism, as part \nof its on -going activities, annually releases print, \nelectronic, online and outdoor media campaigns in  the \ninternational and domestic markets, under the Incredible \nIndia brand-line, to promote various tourism destinations \nand products of the country, including the lesser known \ndestinations which have tourism potential. The Ministry \nof Tourism has been rele asing special campaigns on \nJammu & Kashmir and North East Region on \nDoordarshan and Private TV channels to promote tourism \nin the regions.   \n \n***********\n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO  PARTS (a) AND (b) OF  LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. †750 ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 REGARDING EFFECTS OF \nTERRORISM ON TOURISM. \n \nThe number of Domestic and Foreign Tourist Visits to different States/UTs \nduring  2014 and 2015 \nSl. \nNo. State/UT 2014 2015 \nDomestic Foreign Domestic Foreign \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Island 285146 17235 296684 14674 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 93306974 66333 121591054 237854 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 335974 5204 352067 5705 \n4 Assam 4826702 21537 5491845 24720 \n5 Bihar  22544377 829508 28029118 923737 \n6 Chandigarh 1061419 28365 1073842 29538 \n7 Chhattisgarh 24488465 7777 18327841 6394 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 579638 1799 527782 1797 \n9 Daman & Diu 795167 4620 790911 5858 \n10 Delhi 22626859 2319046 25258051 2379169 \n11 Goa 3544634 513592 4756422 541480 \n12 Gujarat 30912043 235524 36288463 284973 \n13 Haryana 7467064 314757 7395496 303118 \n14 Himachal Pradesh  15924701 389699 17125045 406108 \n15 Jharkhand 33427144 154731 33079530 167785 \n16 J&K 9438544 86477 9145016 58568 \n17 Karnataka  118283220 561870 119863942 636502 \n18 Kerala 11695411 923366 12465571 977479 \n19 Lakshadweep 7315 514 17241 1173 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 63614525 316195 77975738 421365 \n21 Maharashtra 92632097 4389098 103403934 4408916 \n22 Manipur 115499 2769 146169 3260 \n23 Meghalaya 716469 8664 751165 8027 \n24 Mizoram 68203 921 66605 798 \n25 Nagaland 58507 2585 64616 2769 \n26 Odisha 10790622 71426 11786117 66971 \n27 Puducherry 1188093 83291 1297192 106153 \n28 Punjab  24271302 255449 25796361 242367 \n29 Rajasthan 33076491 1525574 35187573 1475311 \n30 Sikkim 562418 49175 705023 38479 \n31 Tamil Nadu 327555233 4657630 333459047 4684707 \n32 Telangana  72399113 75171 94516316 126078 \n33 Tripura 361247 26688 363172 34886 \n34 Uttar Pradesh  182820108 2909735 204888457 3104062 \n35 Uttarakhand 21991315 101966 29496938 105882 \n36 West Bengal 49029590 1375740 70193450 1489500 \n  Total  1282801629 22334031 1431973794 23326163 \nSource: State/Union Territory Tourism Departments \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |751", "t": "Post of Director in IIMs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 751 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nPost of Director in IIMs \n \n751. SHRI ARKA KESHARI DEO: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the post of director has been lying vacant in many Indian Institutes of \nManagement (IIMs) at present; and  \n \n(b)  the total number and names of those IIMs which have initia ted the process of scrutinizing \ndirectors’ posting? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n(a): The posts of Director in the following I ndian Institutes of Management (IIMs) are vacant: \n1.  IIM Bangalore. 8. IIM Amritsar. \n2.  IIM Kozhikode. 9. IIM Sirmaur. \n3.  IIM Rohtak. 10. IIM Bodh Gaya. \n4.  IIM Ranchi. 11. IIM Sambalpur. \n5.  IIM Raipur. 12. IIM Nagpur. \n6.  IIM Udaipur. 13. IIM Visakhapatnam. \n7.  IIM Tiruchirappalli.   \n \nHowever, the Directors of the mentor IIMs are looking after the six new IIMs (IIM \nAmritsar, IIM Sirmaur, IIM Bodh Gaya, IIM Sa mbalpur, IIM Nagpur and IIM Visakhapatnam), \ntill the appointment of regular Director. For othe r IIMs, the tenure of the outgoing Director has \nbeen extended or the senior most Professor of the Institute has been given additional charge of the \npost of Director. \n(b): For all the above IIMs except IIM Kozhikode  and IIM Udaipur, the Search cum Selection \nCommittee has recommended a panel of name s. For IIM Kozhikode and IIM Udaipur \nadvertisement for the posts has been issued.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |752", "t": "National Means-cum-Merit Scholarship Scheme (NMMSS)", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.752 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON  21.11.2016 \n \nNational Means-Cum-Merit Scholarship Scheme (NMMSS) \n752.  KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE:  \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n \n        Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a)  the amount allocated by the Government to the National Means-cum-Merit \nScholarship Scheme (NMMSS) during the last three years and the current financial \nyear; \n(b)   the number of beneficiaries under the said scholarship; and \n(c) whether the Government has disbursed all scholarship and if so, the details thereof \nand if not, the details and the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n \n(a) The amount allocated for the Nati onal Means-cum-Merit Scholarship Scheme \n(NMMSS) during 2013-14, 2014-15 and 2015-16 was Rs. 95 crore, Rs. 70 crore and \nRs. 131.50 crore respectively. For the curr ent financial year 2016- 17, BE is Rs. 35 \ncrore.  \n(b) During the current financial year 2016- 17, the estimated number of beneficiaries \nunder NMMSS Scheme is 2,30,199.  \n(c) Out of 3,37,893 NMMSS scholarshipsdue fo r  disbursement in 2015-16, only \n2,10,012 scholarshipscould be sanctioned during 2015-16,thus leaving 1,27,881 \nscholarships pending for sanction due to non-availability of funds. \n \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |753", "t": "Crime against Foreign Tourists", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.753  \nANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nCRIME AGAINST FOREIGN TOURISTS  \n \n753.  SHRI G.M. SIDDESHWARA:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken no te of the increasing \ncases of crime against tourists at tourist centres in the \ncountry;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c)  the steps taken by the Government in this regard?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a) to (c): The National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB), Ministry of \nHome Affairs, Govt. of India, collects the data on crime against \nforeign tourists since 2014.  They have reported a total number of \n384 and 271 cases of crimes against foreign tourist s during 2014 \nand 2015 respectively showing a decrease of 29.4% during 2015 \nover 2014. NCRB has started collecting data on crimes against \nforeign tourists since 2014 only using MHA approved revised \nproformae of “Crime in India” hence no patterns on such cr imes can \nbe drawn. \n \nCrime categorised as registered , cases charge -sheeted, cases \nconvicted, persons arrested, persons charge -sheeted and persons \nconvicted under crime against foreign tourists during 2014 is at \nAnnexure.   \n \n\nThe measures taken by Ministry of  Tourism to ensure the \nsafety and security of tourists including foreign tourists are as given \nbelow: \n \ni) The Ministry of Tourism has launched the 24x7 Toll Free \nMulti-Lingual Tourist Helpline in 12 Languages including \n10 international languages and in Hindi & English on \n8.2.2016. This service is available on the toll free number \n1800111363 or on a short code 1363 and operational \n24X7 365 days in a year offering a “multi -lingual \nhelpdesk” in the designated languages to provide support \nservice in terms of provi ding information relating to \nTravel & Tourism in India to the domestic and \nInternational tourists and to assist the callers with \nadvice on action to be taken during times of distress \nwhile travelling in India and if need be alert the \nconcerned authorities.  \nii) Adoption of code of conduct by the Indian travel and \ntourism industry for Safe and Honourable Tourism, which \ncontains a set of guidelines to encourage tourism \nactivities to be undertaken with respect to basic rights \nlike dignity, safety and freedom from exploitation of both \ntourists and local residents, in particular women and \nchildren. \n \niii)  All the Chief Ministers of the State Governments and \nAdministrators of Union Territory Administrations have \nbeen asked to take immediate effective steps for \nensuring a conducive and friendly environment for all \ntourists and also request them to publicize the steps \nbeing taken/proposed to be taken to increase the sense \nof security amongst the present/prospective visitors and \nalso to counter the negative publicity, if any. \n \n \n*******\n\nANNEXURE \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.753 ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 REGARDING CRIME AGAINST \nFOREIGN TOURISTS. \n \nCRIME HEAD WISE CASES REGISTERED (CR), CASES CHARGE SHEETED \n(CS), CASES CONVICTED (CV), PERSONS ARRESTED (PAR), PERSONS \nCHARGE SHEETED (PCS) AND PERSONS CONVICTED (PCV) UNDER CRIME \nAGAINST FOREIGN TOURISTS DURING 2014. \nSl. No. Crime Head CR CS CV PAR PCS PCV \n1 Murder 4 2 0 9 8 0 \n2 Attempt to commit Murder 2 3 0 5 6 0 \n3 Culpable Homicide not amounting \nto Murder \n1 0 0 0 0 0 \n4 Attempt to commit Culpable \nHomicide \n0 0 0 0 0 0 \n5 Rape 17 10 0 34 25 0 \n6 Attempt to commit Rape 1 0 0 0 0 0 \n7 Assault on women with intent to \noutrage her Modesty \n33 31 2 30 31 2 \n8 Insult to the Modesty of Women  3 0 0 3 2 0 \n9 Kidnapping & Abduction 1 0 0 1 0 0 \n10 Dacoity 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n11 Robbery 21 10 0 14 14 0 \n12 Grievous Hurt 1 0 0 0 0 0 \n13 Extortion 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n14 Cheating 17 2 0 8 2 0 \n15 Theft 223 36 1 35 36 3 \n16 Forgery 6 1 0 1 1 0 \n17 Importation of Girls from Foreign \nCountry \n0 0 0 0 0 0 \n18 Disclosure of Identity of Victims  0 0 0 0 0 0 \n19 Human Trafficking 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n20 Other IPC 28 16 1 25 19 1 \n21 Immoral Traffic (Prevention) Act, \n1956 \n0 0 0 0 0 0 \n22 Indecent Representation of \nWomen (Prohibition) Act, 1986 \n0 0 0 0 0 0 \n23 Other SLL 26 23 1 25 22 1 \n24 Total Crimes Committed against \nTourist Foreigners \n384 134 5 190 166 7 \nSource: Crime in India \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |754", "t": "Setting up of Float Glass Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT OF INDUSTRIAL POLICY & PROMOTION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 754. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE 21ST NOVEMBER,  2016. \n \nSETTING UP OF FLOAT GLASS PLANTS \n \n754.  SHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL: \nSHRI BHAIRON PRASAD MISHRA: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                    वाणिज्‍ य‍वव ‍द्यग ‍ं ्री \n(a)  the details of the total number of  sheet and plate glass plants operating for  \nbuilding and housing projects in the country at present; \n \n(b)  whether any efforts are being made  by the Government to set up large size  \nfloat glass plants in the country;  \n \n(c)  whether any float glass plant has  suspended its work in the country  \nparticularly at Chitrakut  in Uttar Pradesh,  if so, the details thereof and the \nreasons therefor; and \n \n(d)  the steps taken by the Government to revive the operations of such factories? \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्‍ य‍वव ‍द्यग ‍ााज्‍ यं ्री‍स्‍‍ वतं ्‍्रभााा(स्रींतंरी‍िरंमलाा‍ीरीतंाांि)  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRIMATI NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a): Glass is a delicensed industry. Therefore, it is difficult to provide exact figures. \nAs per  the information available, there are 8 plants for  manufacture of float \nglass. \n(b): No. Glass is a de -licensed industry and under the existing liberalized \neconomic environment - investment decisions for start of new plants are taken \nby entrepreneurs based on techno-economic considerations. \n(c): A glass fa ctory at  Chitrakut was set -up in 1991 by Continental Float Glass \nLtd. Lucknow in collaboration with UP State Mineral Corporation Ltd. They \ncould not start the production.  In 1992, it was decided by Govt. of UP and \nContinental Float Glass Ltd. to close down the factory.  \n(d): No steps are under consideration by Government of India as the factory was \nin Joint collaboration of UP State Mineral Corporation Ltd. \n******  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |755", "t": "Anti-Dumping Duty on Chinese Products", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 755  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st NOVEMBER, 2016 \n \nANTI-DUMPING DUTY ON CHINESE PRODUCTS \n 755.  SHRIMATI VANAROJA R.: \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाͨणÏय एवं उɮयोग मंğी ) be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has imposed a provisional anti dumping duty for six months on \nseamless tubes, steel pipes among others imports from China and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether the imposition of provisional duty is required to offset dumping and injury, \npending completion of investigation and if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Directorate General of Anti-Dumping and Allied Duties had initiated a probe \ninto the alleged dumping and its adverse impact on the domestic industry; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government in this regard? \nANSWER \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh fueZyk lhrkje.k½ ¼Lora= izHkkj½ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SMT. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n(a) to (d):Yes Madam. Directorate General of Anti-Dumping & Allied Duties (DGAD) \ninitiated an anti-dumping investigations on imports of “Seamless tubes, pipes & hollow \nprofiles of iron, alloy or non-alloy steel (other than cast iron and stainless steel), whether \nhot finished or cold drawn or cold rolled of an external diameter not exceeding 355.6 mm \nor 14’’ OD’’, originating in or exported from China PR, on 8.7.2015, on the basis of a \nduly substantiated petition filed by the domestic industry with a prima-facie evidence of \ndumping of said goods into the country causing injury to the domestic industry.  \nDGAD vide its notification dated 31.3.2016 recommended to Department of \nRevenue, for imposition of provisional anti-dumping duty on imports of aforesaid \nproducts imported from China PR to offset dumping and injury, pending completion of \nthis investigation. Department of Revenue, vide its notification No. 18/2016-\nCustoms(ADD) dated 17th May, 2016 has imposed provisional anti-dumping duty in the \nrange of US$ 961.33 to 1610.67 per Metric Ton.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |756", "t": "Promotion of Performing Arts", "head": "Government of India\n \nMinistry of Culture\n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 756\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016\n \n \n \nPROMOTION OF PERFORMING ARTS\n \n \n \n \n \n756. SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA:\n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government has any insti\ntutes and agencies engaged in the \npromotion of performing arts;\n \n \n(\nb\n)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether the Government has allocated any funds for setting up of these \ninstitutes;\n \n \n(\nd\n)\n \nwhether the government plans to set up cultural institutions in the field of \nperforming arts; and\n \n \n(\ne\n)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \n \nAnswer\n \n \nMinister of State (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR CULTURE & TOURISM\n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n(a)to(c)  For promotion of Performing Arts the Government has set up  Sangeet \nNatak Akademi, National School of Dr\nama, Kalakeshtra Foundation and seven \nZonal Cultural Centres (ZCCs) with headquarters at Patiala, Nagpur, Udaipur, \nAllahabad, Kolkata, Dimapur and Thanjavur. Many of these organizations have \nSub\n-\nCentres also.   These organizations are fully funded by the G\novernment and \nreceive grants\n-\nin\n-\naid every year for their establishment and promotional \nactivities for performing arts.\n \n \n(d)\n \n  \nAt present the Government does not have any plan to set up new \ncultural institutions in the field of Performing Arts.\n \n \n(e) \n \n  \nDoes\n \nnot arise.\n \n \n*****\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |757", "t": "Aims and Objectives of Energy Security through Goa Summit", "head": "  LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.757 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST NOVEMBER, 2016 \n \nAIMS AND OBJECTIVES OF ENERGY SECURITY THROUGH GOA SUMMIT \n757.  SHRI K.R.P. PRABAKARAN:  \n                  पैट्रोलियम और प्राकृलिक गैस मंत्री  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) Whether the Government has organised Goa summit to strengthen its energy \nsecurity and long term supplies of hydrocarbons and diversification of imports from \nforeign countries and if so, the details thereof along with a ims and objectives \nachieved through the said summit; \n(b) whether the Government has granted any approval to a consortium of Indian \nenergy companies to buy stakes from foreign countries so far and if so, the details \nthereof; and  \n(c)  whether the Government  has given approval for long pending Russian \nproposal to set up gas supply pipeline to India and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nपैट्रोलियम और प्राकृलिक गैस मंत्रािय में राज्य मंत्री (स्विंत्र प्रभार) (श्री धमेन्द्र प्रधान) \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nPETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n(a) 17th India-Russia Annual Summit was held in Goa on 15.10.2016.  During the \nsummit, following three documents were signed: \n \n(i) MoU between Engineers India Limited and Gazprom on the Joint Study of a \ngas pipeline to India and other possible areas of cooperation. \n(ii) Cooperation Agreement in the area of Education and Training between ONGC \nVidesh Ltd. and Rosneft Oil Company; and \n(iii) Programme of Cooperation (PoC) in the Field of Oil and Gas for the period \n2017-18.   \n(b) Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs ( CCEA) has given approval on \n28.9.2016 for acquisition of 29.9.% stake in LLC Taas -Yuryakh Naftegazodobycha  \n(TAAS) and 23.9% stake in JSC Vankorneft(Vankorneft) by Indian Consortium \ncomprising Oil India Limited (OIL), Indian Oil Corporation Limited (IOCL) and Bharat \nPetroResources Limited (BPRL) from M/s Rosneft Oil Company (Rosneft), the \nNational Oil Company (NOC) of Russian Federation (Russia) and its subsidiary. \n(c) During the India -Russia Annual Summit, and MoU was signed between \nEngineers India Limited and Gazprom on “The Joint Study of a gas pipeline to India \nand other possible areas of cooperation”. \n************** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |758", "t": "Purchases Made through GeM", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 758  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st NOVEMBER, 2016 \n \nPURCHASES MADE THROUGH GeM \n 758.  SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY: \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाͨणÏय एवं उɮयोग मंğी ) be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the items earlier on Directorate General of Supplies and Disposals (DGS&D) rate \ncontract and now on Government e-market (GeM) place are procured mostly not on GeM \nbut against the rate contract concluded by other government agencies like National \nInformatics centre services Incorporated (NICSI) and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) the details of items along with the rates where rates on GeM are/were more than DGS&D \nrates/popular e-commerce sites like Amazon/Snapdeal/ Flipkart; \n(c) the details of purchases of computer made on GeM by Jharkhand Court from treating only \none eligible supplier despite the fact that numbers of known computer brands are \navailable in country and the reasons therefor; and  \n(d) whether rates of computers purchased by Jharkhand Court on GeM were higher than the \nrate contract of DGS&D and if so, the details thereof indicating the increase in percentage \nalong with the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh fueZyk lhrkje.k½ ¼Lora= izHkkj½ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SMT. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a) & (b): Government e-Marketplace (GeM) is a new initiative of Government launched in \nAugust 2016. GeM is expected to bring more transparency and efficiency in public \nprocurement as it is a completely online and end-to-end procurement portal with simplified \nand automated processes for vendor and buyer registration, product/service listing, contract \nplacement & management and time-bound payment to vendors. Since it is a marketplace, it \nprovides a platform for dynamic pricing along with tools for Bidding & Reverse Auction as \nagainst static & long term pricing on NICSI and DGS&D Rate Contract. The rates on GeM are \nfound to be more competitive in comparison between the rate listed  under GeM, NICSI and \nRate Contract has been done in few select Goods and Services in the following table and it \ncould be seen that the rates offered in GeM based on actual order are lesser than NICSI and \nRate Contract. Further, rates offered in GeM are dynamic in nature. \n \n\nItems GeM rate in Rs. DGS&D/NICSI Rate \nContract/Tender in Rs \nSavings % \nLI UPS(.8KVA)/168 \nVAH \n3060 3659(RC Rate) 19.57 \nLI UPS(1)/168 VAH 4043 4278 (RC Rate) 5.81 \nHiring of Swift Dzire \ntaxis on monthly basis \n28000 32500 (Tender rate) 16.07 \nLaser jet printer 7348 8643 RC Rate \n11234 NICSI Rate(3 yr \nwarranty) \n15/34.6 \nPhoto copier Machine 97000 152250 (RC Rate) 56.95 \nPass Book Printer 20890 23312 NISCI rate 10.4 \n \nThe current prices on e-commerce sites are made available on GeM portal for the benefit of \nthe buyers to establish price reasonableness. However, precise comparison cannot be done \non account of different terms & conditions on GeM and e-Commerce sites. It has been \ninvariable noticed that when buyers have gone for Bidding or Reverse Auction, the prices \nobtained are much lower than the e-Commerce sites. Buyers are encouraged to go in for \nBidding/RA in order to derive maximum benefits. \n \n(c): Jharkhand High Court procured 699 numbers of Intel i3 based Desktop Computers \nModel No.RC-31-Dell Optiplex 3040 MT and 233 numbers of Intel i5 based Desktop \nComputers Model No. RC-35-Dell Optiplex 3040 MT through GeM. Jharkhand High Court had \ntaken prior approval of the competent authority for purchasing these specific models of Dell \nmake Desktop computers through GeM. \n \n(d): The Desktop Computers purchased by Jharkhand High Court were not available on \nDGS&D Rate Contract. The Desktop computers available on GeM and purchased by \nJharkhand High Court were of superior 6th Generation processor as against 4th generation \nprocessor of Dell make available on DGS&D RC. Nevertheless, the GeM prices for the \npurchased computers were same as that of the 4th Generation computers on DGS&D RC. \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |759", "t": "Development of Rural India", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 759 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nDevelopment of Rural India \n†759. KUNWAR SARVESH KUMAR: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the higher education institutions in the country are running any \nprogramme or course related to the development of rural India; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to take any steps for running development \nprogramme in order to connect the Government educational and technical \ninstitutions with rural areas in the country; and if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether any efforts are being made in this regard; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n(a) to (e) : Yes, Madam. The Ministry of  Human Resource Development (MHRD) has \nlaunched Unnat Bharat Abhiyan (UBA) on 11 th November, 2014 to enable higher \neducational institutions to work with the people of rural India in identifying development \nchallenges and evolving appropriate solution s for accelerating sustainable growth. All \nhigher educational institutions, which are funded by the Central/ State Governments and \nother eminent educational institutions have been advised to adopt clusters of Gram \nPanchayats in their vicinity, apply their knowledge and technical expertise to assist the \nlocal bodies/ district administration to formulate qualitative development plans for \nimplementation and subsequent improvement in the quality of rural life. \nIndian Institute of Technology (IIT) Delhi is presently the coordinating institute for Unnat \nBharat Abhiyan. A National Steering Committee has been constituted comprising eminent \nscientist, technologists, educationists as well as representatives from six different \nMinistries of Government of India. Thirteen other premier institutions of Science, \nTechnology and higher education have been enrolled to coordinate and engage in village \ndevelopment exercise in different regions of the country. Ninety Eight districts in the \ncountry have been identified for Phase-I of the programme. Twelve of the institutions are \nworking as Subject Expert Group (SEGs) on various development aspects. Their resources \nand know-how will be accessible to other institutions working in the villages. The details \nof the programme are available on UBA’s website http://unnat.iitd.ac.in/index.php/en/ . \n******** \n\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |76", "t": "Upgradation of Railway Station", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.76 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 16.11.2016 \n \nUPGRADATION OF RAILWAY STATION \n \n76.  DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n             \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether Kazhakootam  railway station (KZK) caters to the \ntechnopark, which employ s more than 40,000 Information \nTechnology (IT) professionals and the Vikram Sarabhi Space \nCenter; \n \n(b) if so, whether the Government would consider adopting measures \nto upgrade the station in collabo ration with the Ministry of \nCommunications and Information Technology with source funding \nfrom technopark companies; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(d) whether the Railways would consider upgrading KZK to 'Adarsh' \nstatus and ren ame the station to Kazhakootam - Technopark \nstation; and \n \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI RAJEN GOHAIN) \n \n(a):  Yes, Madam.  Kazhakuttam is the nearest st ation to Technopark \nand Vikram Sarabhai Space Centre.   \n\n(b) & ( c): Upgradation of station is a continuous and on -going process \nand works in this regard are undertaken depending upon need, volume of \nthe passenger traffic and inter-se priority subject to availability of funds. \nHowever, no  such proposal has been received by Railways from the \nMinistry of Communication and Technology. \n(d) & (e): Kazhakuttam Railway station has been identified for \ndevelopment under Adarsh station Scheme and the related   works under \nthis scheme have been compl eted during 2015 -16.  As per the existing \nguidelines, Ministry of Home Affairs, Government of India, alone is the \ncompetent authority to approve change in the name of railway station on \nIndian Railways. It does so on the recommendation of the concerned \nState Government.   \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |760", "t": "Khadi Artisans Welfare Trust Fund", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 760\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016\n \n \nKHADI ARTISANS WELFARE TRUST FUND\n \n \n760. \n \nSHRIMATI  MEENAKASHI  LEKHI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state: \n \n(a)\n \n \nthe aims and objectives of the Khadi Artisans Welfare Trust Fund (KAWTF);\n \n(b)\n \n \nthe number of States/UTs currently covered under this fund; and\n \n(c)\n \n \nthe cumulative balance v\nalue under the fund at present?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF\n \n \nMICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH)\n \n \n(a)\n:\n \nKhadi\n \nArtisans Welfare Fund Trust was started for implementation of social security \nmeasures such as medical, educational, maternity, construction of house, assistance for \nburial/funeral etc. for artisans associated with Khadi Institutions. These are setup at S\ntate \nlevel. As per the Trust Guidelines Khadi Institutions collect 12% of Artisans’ earning as their \ncontribution to AWF Trust and deposit the amount alongwith matching contribution of the \ninstitution to the Trusts. The Trust Funds are administered by the \nTrust authority constituted \nin accordance with bye\n-\nlaws of Trusts.\n \n \n(b)&(c)\n:\n \nSo far, 21 State level Khadi Artisans Welfare Fund Trusts have been setup in \nthe country with a cumulative fund of Rs.86.63 crore available with the Trusts. The details of \nArtisan\ns Welfare Fund Trusts and funds available with them as on 31.03.2016 is given in \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n. \n \n \n* * *\n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nAnnexure\n-\nI referred to in reply to part (\nb\n)\n&\n(c) of the Lok Sabha \nUns\ntarred \nQuestion No. \n760\n \nfor answer on 21.11.2016\n \n \nDetails of Artisans Welfare Fund Trusts and funds available with them as on 31.03.2016 \n \n \n \n(Rs. in lakh)\n \nSl. \nNo\n \nState/Divisional \noffices\n \nName of the AWFT\n \nDeposit \nfunds \navailable\n \n1.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nAssam\n \nKhadi & Polyvastra\n \nArtisan Welfare and Pension Trust, c/o \nUttar Purvanchal Khadi Gramodyog Maha Sangh, Llmati, P.O. \nBasistha, Kamroop, Guwahati \n–\n \n781 029. \n \n27.49\n \n2.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nAmbala Cant. \n(Haryana)\n \nKhadi & Polyvastra\n \nArtisan Welfare fund and Pension Trust, \nc/o The Pooja Khadi Gramodyog Samiti, Patti, Kalyan, Dist.\n-\nPanipat.\n \n171.49\n \n3.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nShimla (H.P.)\n \nH. P. Khadi & Polyvastra Artisan Welfare Pension Trust / \nSociety, c/o State office, KVIC, 5\n-\nDingle Est\nate, Cart Road, \nShimla \n–\n \n171 004. \n \n31.20\n \n4.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nJammu (J&K)\n \nKhadi & Polyvastra Artisan Welfare and Pension Trust, c/o \nGandhi Bhavan, Jammu.\n \n46.82\n \n5.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nJaipur (Raj.)\n \nKhadi & Polyvastra\n \nArtisan Welfare and Pension Trust, c/o \nRajasthan Khadi Gramodyog Sanstha Sangh, Rajaji Nagar, \nJaipur.\n \n597.84\n \n6.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nChandigarh (Punjab)\n \nKhadi Avem Polyvastra Artisan Welfare and Pension Trust, \nc/o Kshetraya Panjab Khadi Mandal\n, Chandigarh, Kharar \nDist., Ropar.\n \n45.66\n \n7.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nKolkata (W.B.)\n \nKhadi & Polyvastra Artisan Welfare and Pension Trust, 70 A, \nShyampukur Street, Kolkata \n–\n \n700 004. \n \n1429.28\n \n8.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nPatna (Bihar)\n \nKhadi & Polyvastra Shilpi Avem \nKalyan Avem Pension Trust, \nc/o Gram Nirman Mandal, Khadi Gramodyog Samiti, \nJayaprakash Nagar, Buniyad Ganje, Gaya, Patna, Bihar \n \n10.73\n \n9.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nRanchi (Jharkhand)\n \nState Level Khadi & Polyvastra Kamgar Kalyan Fund Trust. \n \n40.71\n \n10.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nBhubaneshwar \n(Odisha)\n \nOdisha Khadi & Polyvastra Artisan Welfare fund and Pension \nTrust, Kedarlane, Bhubaneshwar \n-\n \n \n34.70\n \n11.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nHyderabad (A.P.)\n \nKhadi & Polyvastra Artisan Welfare and Pension Trust, c/o \nThe Guntur \nZilla Khadi Gramodyog Samstha, 6/1 \nChandramouli Nagar, Guntur \n–\n \n7, Andhra Pradesh  \n \n37.02\n \n12.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nBangalore (KNT)\n \nKhadi & Polyvastra Artisan Welfare and Pension Trust, c/o \nKarnataka Sarvodaya Sangh, Rangarao Road, Sankarapuram, \nBangalore \n–\n \n560 004  \n \n159.46\n \n13.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nChennai (T. N.) \n \nTamilnadu State Khadi & Polyvastra Artisan Welfare  and \nPension Trust, c/o Tamilnadu Sarvodaya Sangh, Campur \nGandhinagar, Tirupur \n–\n \n641 603  \n \n157.59\n \n14.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nMaharashtra Khadi & Polyvastra Artisan Welfare and Pension \n113.63\n \n\nMumbai (Mah.)\n \nAssociation, c/o Maharashtra Seva Sangh, 727, Sadasiva Peth, \nPune \n–\n \n411 030 \n \n \n15.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nAhmedabad (Guj.)\n \nKhadi & Polyvastra Artisan Welfare and Pension Trust, c/o \nKasturba Wad\ni, Nr, Balalmandir, Gandhi Ashram, Ahmedabad \n–\n \n380 027 \n \n1721.26\n \n16.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nBhopal (M.P.)\n \nM.P. Khadi & Polyvastra Artisan Welfare and Pension Trust, \nc/o Gandhi Bhawan Complex, Shyamla Hills, Bhopal \n–\n \n462 \n002 \n \n61.15\n \n17.\n \nState office, KVIC, \nRaipur (Chhatisgarh)\n \nKhadi & Polyvastra Artisan Welfare and Pension Trust, \nRaipur, Chhattisgarh \n \n60.63\n \n18.\n \nState office, KVIC, \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |761", "t": "Setting up of Industries", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT OF INDUSTRIAL POLICY & PROMOTION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 761. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE 21ST NOVEMBER,  2016. \n \n \nSETTING UP OF INDUSTRIES \n \n761.  SHRI HARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                   वाणिज्‍ य‍वव ‍द्यग ‍ं ्री \n(a)  the details of financial and nonfinancial  assistance given by the Union  Government \nto industrial establishments  to set up new industry in the  country including Nasik \ndistrict of Maharashtra during the last three years, State-wise; \n \n(b)  the number of such industrial  establishment of Nashik district of  Maharashtra which \nhave availed the benefit of this assistance during the said period, State-wise; and \n \n(c)  the details of production by the said industrial establishment during the said period? \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्‍ य‍वव ‍द्यग ‍ााज्‍ यं ्री‍स्‍‍ वतं ्‍्रभााा(स्रींतंरी‍िरंमलाा‍ीरीतंाांि(‍ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRIMATI NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a) : Under the liberalized industrial policy regime, the investors take the decision about \nlocating new industries in a State. Further, except for specific financial and non -financial \nincentives given as per Scheme parameters under Sectoral and Spatial Plan Schemes of \nvarious Ministries, the Central Government does not provide financial assistance for \nsetting up of new Industries in any State.  The Government makes efforts through policy \nmeasures to encour age setting up of industries in various sectors and in difficult areas.  \nFor helping industries in difficult areas for inclusive industrial growth/development, the \nPlan Schemes  viz. special package for Special Category States (Jammu & Kashmir, \nHimachal Pra desh and Uttarakhand), North -East Industrial and Investment Promotion \nPolicy (NEIIPP) 2007 for the States of North Eastern Region and Sikkim, Transport \nSubsidy Scheme, 1971/Freight Subsidy Scheme, 2013 (for the States of Himachal \nPradesh, Uttarakhand, J&K,  Darjeeling district of West Bengal, Andaman and Nicobar \nIslands, Lakshadweep and 8 North Eastern States)   are in operation.  Apart from this, \nassistance is provided under  Industrial Infrastructure Upgradation Scheme (IIUS), 2003/ \nModified Industrial Infr astructure Upgradation Scheme (MIIUS), 2013  and Integrated \nLeather Development Programme (ILDP) Schemes of Department of industrial Policy and \nPromotion. \n \n(b) & (c):The project proposals from States as per Scheme parameters under Sectoral and Spatial \nPlan Schemes of various Ministries are received and processed by the Ministries \nconcerned, details of production by such specific industrial establishments is not centrally \nmaintained in Department of Industrial Policy and Promotion. \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |762", "t": "Investment under NELP", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.762 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st November, 2016  \n \n \nINVESTMENT UNDER NELP \n \n762.    SHRI KALIKESH N. SINGH DEO: \n \nपेट्रोलियम और प्राक ृ लिक गैस मंत्री \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)   the details of oil and gas blocks offered/allotted to State owned Public Sector oil \ncompanies and private companies under New Exploration and Licensing Policy (NELP) in \nthe eighth and ninth round of auction, company-wise; \n(b)  whether the majority of investmen t under NELP has been made by Public Sector Oil \ncompanies or by private companies if so, the details thereof along with the steps taken by \nthe Government to attract more investments from Public Sector oil companies; \n \n(c)    the number of foreign companies that participated in the last three rounds of auction \nand the reasons for the participation in the last round, company-wise; and \n \n(d)  the corrective action taken by the Government to boost participation in tenth round of \nauction by Public Sector oil companies? \n \n \n \nANSWER \nपेट्रोलियम और प्राक ृ लिक गैस मंत्रािय में राज्य मंत्री (प्राार रन्त्िवत्र( )प्रााप्र ामेवध ीी)  \nMINISTER OF STATE (I/C) IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a):   Details of oil and gas blocks awarded to State owned Public Sector oil companies \nand private companies under New Exploration and Licensing Policy (NELP) in the eighth \nand ninth round of auction, company-wise are at Annexure  \n(b):   The details of investment toward exploration and development activities under \nNELP made are as under \n                                                                                                                 In Million US$ \nOperator Type Development Exploratory Grand Total \nPSU 1870.38 10211.37 12081.75 \nPrivate 7822.22 4547.12 12369.34 \nForeign 19.68 1264.20 1283.88 \nGrand Total 9712.29 16022.68 25734.98 \n      \nIn order to attract more investment in E& P sector, Government has approved a new \nHydrocarbon Exploration and Licensing Policy (HELP) for exploration and production of oil \nand gas which replaces existing New Exploration Licensing Policy (NELP). This policy \nprovides for a uniform licensing system to explore and produce all hydrocarbons such as oil, \n\ngas, coal bed methane, shale oil/gas, etc. under a single licensing framework Policy also \nprovides many incentives such as reduced royalty rates for offshore blocks, marketing & \npricing freedom and easy to administer revenue sharing model \n (c) & (d):   35 foreign companies participated in the last three round s i.e VII, VIII, and IV \nrounds of bidding. NELP was introduced in 1997 to provide a level playing fields to Foreign, \nPrivate and Public  Sector Companies as well as to attract investments and technologies in \nE&P Sector. Under NELP, blocks were awarded through a transparent international \ncompetitive bidding process. Government has notified on 30th March 2016, a new \nHydrocarbon and Exploration Licensing Policy (HELP) in place of NELP. \n \n******* \n  \n\nAnnexure  \nAnnexure-I of reply to Lok Sabha USQuestion No. 762 for Answer on 21.11.2016 \nOperator \nType Operator \nNELP Round \nGrand Total NELP VIII NELP IX \nForeign \nBengal Energy International Inc. 1   1 \nBHP Billiton Pty. Ltd. 3   3 \nBritish Gas Exploration and Production (India) Ltd. 1 1 2 \nCairn Energy India Pty Ltd. 2   2 \nDeep Energy Llc \n \n3 3 \nForeign Total 7 4 11 \nPrivate \nFocus Energy Ltd. \n \n1 1 \nHarish Chandra (India) Ltd. 2   2 \nJay Polychem (India) Ltd. 1   1 \nJubilant Oil & Gas Private Limited. 2   2 \nPan India Consultants \n \n1 1 \nPratibha Oil and Natural Gas Pvt.  Ltd. \n \n1 1 \nPrize Petroleum Company Ltd. \n \n1 1 \nSankalp Oil and Natural Resources Ltd. \n \n1 1 \nEsveegee Steel (Gujarat) Pvt Ltd 3   3 \nPrivate Total 8 5 13 \nPSU \nGAIL (India) Limited \n \n2 2 \nNational Thermal Power Corporation 1   1 \nOil and Natural Gas Corporation Ltd. 14 5 19 \nOil India Ltd. 2 3 5 \nPSU Total 17 10 27 \nGrand Total 32 19 51 \n \n  \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |763", "t": "Wages of Agricultural Labourers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n LOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 763 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nWAGES OF AGRICULTURAL LABOURERS \n \n†763. DR. SWAMI SAKSHIJI MAHARAJ: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)the level of wages of agricultural labourers in various States in the country \nas on 31 July, 2016, State/UT-wise; \n(b)whether the State Governments are revising such wages in every five years \nas mandated under the law and if so, the details thereof alon g with the \ncompliance, State/UT-wise; \n(c)the amount of wages of agricultural labourers increased during the last \nthree years and the current year State/UT-wise; and \n(d)the other steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government in this \nregard? \n \n  ANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n(a) & (b): Under the Minimum Wages Act, 1948 both the Central and the State \nGovernments are appropriate Governments to fix and revise minimum wages \nevery five years in respect of scheduled employments within their \njurisdictions. The scheduled employment “Agriculture” falls both in the Central \nand the State Sphere. \n \nAs a part of Rural Labour Enquiry, Labour Bureau has been \ncompiling and maintaining average daily wage rates in respec t of selected \nagricultural and non -agricultural occupations on the basis of the information \ncollected by the National Sample Survey Organization from a fixed set of 600 \nsample villages spread over 20 States.  Average daily wage rates for General \nAgricultural Labourers in rural areas for the month of July, 2016 is given at \nAnnexure I. \n \n(c) & (d):      A statement showing State-wise Annual Average Wage Rates and \ntheir Growth Rates 