ED |766", "t": "Establishment of Telugu Vidyapeeth University", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 766 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nEstablishment of Telugu Vidyapeeth University \n \n766. SHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY:  \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Ministry has received any proposal from State Government of Andhra Pradesh \nrequesting for establishment of Telugu Vidyapeeth in Guntur;  \n \n(b) If so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) Whether any action has been initiated by the Ministry in this regard; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n(a) to (d): At present there is no proposal under consideration to set up a new Central University \nby the name of Telugu Vidyapeeth in Guntur. However, the Andhra Pradesh Re-organisation Act \n2014 inter alia provides for establishment of on e Central University and one Central Tribal \nUniversity in Andhra Pradesh. \n \nDuring 12th Plan (2012-17), as approved by National Development Council (NDC), the thrust is \non consolidation of the higher education system . Expansion would be mainly done by scaling up \ncapacity of the existing institutions in place of setting up new institutions. The Central \nGovernment has launched Rashtriya Uchchatar Shiksha Abhiyan (RUSA) on cost sharing basis to \nassist States to open new institutions. \n \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |767", "t": "Reduction in Taxes to Increase and Promote Tourism", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.767  \nANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nREDUCTION IN TAXES TO INCREASE AND PROMOTE TOURISM  \n \n767.  SHRI R. PARTHIPAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Governm ent is planning to announce liberal \npolicies and reduction in taxes along with a comprehensive \npackage for attracting tourist and foreign investment in the \ncountry;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether the Government is promoting India as a bran d in \ntourism sector;  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e)  whether any positive indications are witnessed in this regard \nand if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a): Announcement of such policies and taxation proposals are \ndealt with as part of the Budget. The Ministry of Tourism has so far \nnot given any such input for the Union Budget 2017 -18.  \n \n(b): Does not arise in view of the reply at (a) above.  \n \n(c) and (d): The Ministry of  Tourism is promoting India as a tourism \ndestination by releasing campaigns in international and domestic \nmarkets and by undertaking several promotional activities through \nits Indiatourism Offices overseas under the Incredible India brand -\nline. The campaig ns are released through various advertising \nmediums such as Print, Television, Online and Outdoor. Indiatourism \n\nOffices overseas participate in various international travel fairs and \nexhibitions and also undertake numerous promotional activities for \npositioning India as a 365 days tourism destination in major source \nmarkets around the world. \n \n(e): Yes, Madam. The Foreign Exchange Earnings (FEEs) from the \ntourism sector and Foreign Tourist Arrivals (FTAs) have gone up for \nthe last three years as given below:  \n \nYear FEEs (in Rs. crore) FTAs (Nos) \n2013 1,07,671 69,67,601 \n2014 1,23,320 76,79,099 \n2015 1,35,193 80,27,133 \n \n******** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |768", "t": "Copyright Board", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT OF INDUSTRIAL POLICY & PROMOTION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 768. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE 21ST NOVEMBER,  2016. \n \n \nCOPYRIGHT BOARD \n \n768.  DR. GOKARAJU GANGA RAJU: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                  वाणिज्‍ य‍वव ‍द्यग ‍ं ्री \n(a)  whether it is a fact that the  Government has started the process for  setting up \nthe copyright Board in the country; and \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with  the time by which it is likely to be  \nestablished? \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्‍ य‍वव ‍द्यग ‍ााज्‍ यं ्री‍स्‍‍ वतं ्‍्रभााा(स्रींतंरी‍िरंमलाा‍ीरीतंाांि(‍ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRIMATI NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n(a): Yes, Madam. \n \n(b): The administration of the Copyright Act, 1957 was transferred to Department \nof Industrial Policy & Promotion (DIPP), Ministry of Commerce and Industry, \nsubsequent to the amendment in the Government of In dia (Allocation of \nBusiness) Rules, 1961 on 21 st  March, 2016. The department has amended \nsub-rule (1) of rule 3 of the Copyright Rules, 2013 related to the “terms and \nconditions of the office of the Chairman and Members of the Copyright Board” \nvide gazette notification dated 12 th August, 2016. The department has taken \nfurther necessary steps for the appointment of Chairman, Copyright Board. \nVacancy circular/ advertisement for the appointment of two Members of \nCopyright Board has also been published in the Employment News dated 05th \nNovember, 2016. The Copyright Board is likely to be established during the \nnext financial year 2017-18. \n                  \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |769", "t": "Udyog Aadhaar Memorandum", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 769\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON:  21.11.2016\n \n \nUDYOG AADHAAR MEMORANDUM\n \n \n769. \n \n \nDR. SHASHI THAROOR:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government is aware that even though the filing of the Entrepreneur \nMemorandum (EM) and issuance of Acknowledgement has been replaced by Udyog Aadhaar \nMemorandum (UA\nM) and Udyog Aadhaar Acknowledgement respectively, a majority of \nMSMEs with EM have not obtained their UAM, if so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nwhether the Government propose to make the UAM mandatory for accessing benefits \nunder the Government schemes, if so, th\ne details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether it is a fact that non\n-\nregistration of MSMEs is a major cause for the non\n-\nimplementation of various development schemes, including the Prime Minister Employment \nGeneration Programme (PMEGP), which\n \nseek to generate employment and strengthen the \nMSMEs, if so, the details thereof; and\n \n \n(\nd\n)\n \nwhether the Government has adopted measures to motivate new MSMEs to register \nand obtain a UAM and Udyog Aadhaar Acknowledgement if so, the details thereof?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF\n \n \nMICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI HARIBHAI PARATHIBHAI CHAUDHARY)\n \n \n(a) to (d):    Filing of Entrepreneur Memorandum(EM) has been replaced by Udyog Aadhaar \nMemorandum(UAM). Further, UAM registration is an on lin\ne one\n-\npage exercise which does \nnot take more than 5 minutes. Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises(MSME) \nnotified the Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises Development (Furnishing of Information) \nRules, 2016 vide Gazette Notification of GSR 750(E) \ndated 29\nth\n \nJuly, 2016 mandating \nfurnishing of information in MSME Data Bank by all MSMEs. It is mandatory for accessing \nbenefits of various schemes of the Ministry of MSME.\n \n \nUnder the Prime Minister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP), assistance is \nprovided for setting up of an enterprise. UAM registration could only be taken after an \nenterprise is set up. The Ministry of MSME has been taking up with the various State \nGovernment, Industry Associations, its own field offices and through the field offi\nces of the \nNational Small Industries Corporation Limited(NSIC) for encouraging the registration of \nUAM and getting entries made in MSME Databank.\n \n********\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |77", "t": "Sports facility IES in smart city mission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 77 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON NOVEMBER 16, 2016 \n \nSPORTS FACILITY IES IN SMART CITY MISSION \n \nNo. 77     SHRI RAVNEET SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government propos es to provide ultra-modern \nsports facilities in selected smart cities; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Ludhiana Municipal Corporat ion has included \nthe construction of a similar sports facility under its smart-\ncity proposal; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the manner in which the \nremaining facilities can be included in smart cities? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN \nTHE MINITRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(RAO INDERJIT SINGH) \n \n \n(a) & (b): No, Madam. The core infrastructure elements in a \nSmart City do not include modern sports facilities. However, \nif a particular smart city wants to develop modern sports \nfacilities, the Mission Guidelines do not preclude this. \n   \n(c):  No, Madam. Ludhiana Muni cipal Corporation has not \nproposed for construction of spo rts facility under its smart \ncity proposal. \n \n(d): Does not arise. \n \n………..  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |770", "t": "Yashpal Committee on Education", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 770 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nYashpal Committee on Education \n \n†770.  SHRI SADASHIV LOKHANDE: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken/ propos ed to take any steps to review the slow \nprogress of implementation of the Yashpal Co mmittee report regarding easing out education \nburden of school children;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof as on date; and  \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE \nDEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n \n(a) to  (c)  The issues related to curriculu m load raised in the Yash Pal Committee Report \n(1993) on ‘Learning Without Burden’ were, inter-alia, taken into consideration at the time of \nframing of the National Curriculum Framework (NCF) –2005 by the National Council for \nEducational Research and Training (NCERT).   The NCF sets the guidelines and direction \nfor the development of syllabi and textbooks at all the school stages. As a follow up to the \nNCF, curriculum, syllabi, text-books and ot her supplementary material are developed by \nNCERT.   State Council of Educational Research and Training (SCERTs) and State Education \nBoards either adopt or adapt NCERT’s mode l syllabi and textbooks or develop their own \nsyllabi and textbooks based on NCF.  Education, be ing a subject in the Concurrent List of the \nConstitution, it is for the respective States/Uni on Territory Governments to prescribe norms \nwith regard to appropriate weight of School Bags for their schools.  \n\n \n \n \nThe National Council of Educational Research and Training (NCERT) and Central Board of \nSecondary Education (CBSE) ha ve already taken measures to  reduce the weight of school \nbags. NCERT has not recommended any text book for early childhood education. It has \nrecommended only two books (Language and Mathematics) for classes I & II and three books \nfor classes III to IV (Language, Environmenta l Studies and Mathematics).  The number and \nsize of NCERT’s textbooks used by the pr imary and secondary school students are \nappropriate to their age. NCER T has also made available all their textbooks for free access \nthrough the web (epathshala.nic.in) and mobile devices.  The collections include textbooks for \nall subjects across all the stag es and made available for free download and dissemination. \nCBSE has directed schools affiliated to it to ensure that students do not carry school bags till \nclass II and also to restrict the number of books to  be prescribed in classes I-VIII.  It has also \nissued a circular on 20.7.2015 to ensure that th e parents are not forc ed to buy excessive \nnumber of textbooks. \n \n \n**** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |771", "t": "Funds Allocated National Archives of India", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.77\n1 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n21\nst\n \nNOVEMBER\n, 2016\n \nFUND\nS\n \nALLOCATED NATIONAL ARCHIVES OF INDIA\n \n77\n1.    SHRI\nMATI POONAM MAHAJAN\n:\n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a)\n \n \nthe fund\ns allocated by the Government for the National  Archives of India during \nthe last five years; \n \n \n(b) \nthe methods implemented by the ministry for the promotion and accessibility of \nnational archives to the people of the country and for foreigners;\n \n(c)\n \nthe ra\nte of enrolment of people/stude\nnts into the School of Archival\n \nStudies and \ndetails thereof;\n \n(d) \nwhether the Government provides serv\nices of archival studies at all\n \nregional \noffices of National Archives of India; and\n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (I/C) FOR CULTURE AND TOURISM \n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n(a)     \n \nLast five years fund Allocated to National Archives of India\n \n(Rs. In Lakhs)\n \nInclusive \nyear\n \nPlan\n \nNon\n-\nPlan\n \nBE\n \nRE\n \nBE\n \nRE\n \n2012\n-\n13\n \n900.00\n \n1070.00\n \n1720.00\n \n2379.50\n \n2013\n-\n14\n \n1100.0\n0\n \n1400.00\n \n1918.00\n \n1793.50\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n2350.00\n \n1202.44\n \n1842.90\n \n1930.15\n \n2015\n-\n16\n \n2225.00\n \n2130.00\n \n2056.00\n \n2116.10\n \n2016\n-\n17\n \n1770.00\n \n1470.00\n \n2009\n.\n10\n \n2009.10\n \n \n \n(b)  \n  \n1. \n \nThe Departmental website \nwww.nationalarchives\n.nic.in\n \nprovides information \n  \non the holdings, access policy, timings and services provided by NAI which \nis a tool for promotion of the activities of the National Archives of India.\n \n \n1.1 \n \nIn addition to this, an online search portal \nwww.Abhilekh\n-\nPatal.in\n \nlaunched in \n   \nMarch 2015 gives updated information on the records and reference media \nbeing digitized and uploaded by the Department from time to time.\n \n \nContd…2\n \n\n-\n2\n-\n \n \n1.2 \nThe Sardar Patel webportal \nhttp://sardarpatel.nvli.in/\n \nis an effort of the \nNational Virtual Library of India (under the National Mission of Libraries, \nMinistry of Culture), to bring to the public a vast collection of archival \nmaterial. \n \n \n \n1.3 \n \nWebportal o\nn Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose \nhttp://www.netajipapers.gov.in\n \nis \nanother initiative of the Department promoting the archival holdings and their \naccessibility.\n \n \n    \n1.4 \n  \nMany outreach programmes like exhibitions a\nnd seminars also promote the \narchival heritage in the custody of the National Archives of India.\n \n \n    \n1.5  \nPresence of NAI on the social media platforms also helps in promoting the \nactivities of NAI.\n \n \n  \n2.\n \nNational Archives of India is the custodian of all\n \n`Public` records of \nGovernment of India and these are available for use of `bonafide’ research as \nper the provisions of the Public Records Act, 1993. Access to the records in \nNational Archives of India is governed by the provisions of Public Records \nRules\n, 1997.\n \n \n \n     \n2.1  As per sub\n-\nsection (2) of Section 11 of the Public Records Rules, 1997,\n \n \n“A \nperson who intends to consult the public records shall apply to the Director \nGeneral or Head of the Archives, as the case may be, in the prescribed \n \nForm\n-\n8”\n.\n \n \n2\n.2  \nUnder sub\n-\nsection (3) of Section 11 of the Public Record Rules, 1997, \nforeign nationals intending to consult the public records at the National \nArchives of India are permitted on the production of letters of introduction \nfrom their sponsoring instituti\non and Diplomatic Mission in addition to \nfilling up Form\n-\n8.\n \n \n(c\n)   \n  \n1.\n \nApplication fee for all the courses\n \na\n)\n \nRupees 100.00 for Indian applicants and applicants from SAARC \ncountries; \n \nb\n)\n \nUS $ 40.00 for applicants from other foreign countries. \n \n \n2\n.\n \nCourse fee\n \n \na\n)\n \nRupe\nes 1500.00 for trainees from India and SAARC countries and \n    \nUS $ 500.00 for trainees from other countries for One Year Diploma \ncourse in Archives and Records Management;\n \nb\n)\n \nRupees 300.00 for trainees from India and SAARC countries and \n      \nUS $ 300.00 for\n \ntrainees from other countries for short term certificate \ncourses of six weeks and eight weeks duration; \n \nc\n)\n \nRupees 200.00 for trainees from India and SAARC countries and \n      \nUS $ 200.00 for trainees from other countries for short term certificate \ncourses o\nf four weeks duration. \n \n \n(d) \n \nNo, Madam.\n \n \n(e)  \n \nNot applicable in view of (d) above.\n \n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |772", "t": "PSUs Working under the Ministry", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.772   \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st NOVEMBER, 2016 \n \nPSUS WORKING UNDER THE MINISTRY \n 772.  SHRI INNOCENT: \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाͨणÏय एवं उɮयोग मंğी ) be \npleased to state: \n(a) the names and locations of Central Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs) working under the \nMinistry; \n(b) the number of contract/casual labourers appointed therein during the last three years \nindicating the areas/jobs in which these contract/casual labourers were appointed;  \n(c) the number of contract/casual labourers who were regularized during the said period \nalong with the mechanism adopted to ensure minimum wages to these workers; \n(d) the mechanism put in place to ensure that the provisions of various labour laws are not \nviolated by contractors and others; and \n(e) the action taken in cases of complaints against the violations by contractors? \nANSWER \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh fueZyk lhrkje.k½ ¼Lora= izHkkj½ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SMT. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a) The names and location of Central Public Sector Undertakings (CPSUs)  \nunder Ministry of Commerce & Industry are as under:- \nS.No. Name  of PSU Location \n1. MMTC Ltd. Core-1, CGO Complex, Lodi Road, New Delhi \n2. State Trading Corporation of \nIndia(STC) Ltd. \nJawaharVayaparBhawan, Tolstoy Marg, New Delhi \n3. PEC Ltd. Hansalaya, 15 Barakhamba Road, New Delhi \n4. STCL Limited “Chandrodaya”, No.10/1, 2 nd Main, 30th cross, 7th \nBlock, Jayanagar, Bangalore 5600 070 (Karnataka) \n5. Indian Trade Promotion \nOrganisation (ITPO) \nPragatiBhawan, PragatiMaidan, New Delhi \n6. ECGC Ltd. 10 th Floor, Express Towers, Nariman Point, Mumbai- \n400021 \n \n \n(b) The services of contract workers/labourers are generally engaged by the \nPSUs through various contractors/agencies for security, housekeeping, \n\nhorticulture, pantry, electrical maintenance, drivers, etc.  The details of \nnumber of contract workers engaged during the last three years by the \nPSUs under this Ministry is as follows:-  \nPSU 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \nMMTC 286 283 296 \nSTC 293 314 322 \nPEC 77 48 49 \nSTCL 0 0 0 \nITPO 0 0 0 \nECGC 69 73 104 \nTOTAL 725 718 771 \n \n(c)to(e): No contract worker was regularised during the said period.  At the time of \nagreement with outsourcing agencies, the PSUs ensure that the contractor \nhas a valid licence and also registration with the ESI and EPF \norganisations and execute a written agreement with the contractor \ncovering scope of job, legal obligation, payment terms, other terms and \nconditions.  As per the payment clause, a contractor prefers his monthly \ncontribution paid for the workers. In case of non-compliance of contractual \nobligations by contractors, the contract provides for pre-mature termination \nof contract. \n  \n During last three years, no complaint has been received by PSUs against \nthe violation by agencies in respect of statutory compliance except MMTC \nRegional Office (RO), Kolkata.  On instruction of Regional Office Kolkata, \nthe contractor has since taken corrective actions.  \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |773", "t": "Setting up of Libraries", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 773 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nSETTING UP OF LIBRARIES \n \n \n† 773.  SHRIMATI RANJANBEN BHATT \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is considering to set up Libraries in various parts of the country \nin the name of Pt. Deen Dayal Upadhyay; \n(b) if so, whether the Government is also considering opening of  such libraries in Gujarat \nalso;  \n (c) the number and names of such places where these libraries are likely to be set up; \n              (d) whether assistance would be provided to interested private individuals also under the said   \ninitiative to open libraries and if so, the details thereof including the amount of funds \nlikely to be provided; and  \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n       \nANSWER \n       MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT  \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n \n(a)            : No Madam. \n(b) to (e):   Doesn’t arise. \n \n \n \n                                                                          *** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |774", "t": "Open Universities", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.774 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st NOVEMBER, 2016 \n \nOpen Universities  \n \n†774. SHRI R. DHRUVA NARAYANA:  \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has ac corded approval to the State open  universities for running M. \nPhil. and Ph.D. courses;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the State open universities have given affidavits in this regard; and  \n(d) if so, the number of such universities in the States/UTs? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n         (DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n(a to d):  As per the information furnished by  the University Grants  Commission (UGC) that \nbased on the request of the Vice- Chancellors of the following four (04) Open & Distance \nLearning (ODL) mode universities, the Commission has permitted them to conduct M.Phil/Ph.D. \nprogramme strictly in accordan ce with the UGC (Minimum Sta ndards and Procedure for Award \nof M.Phil/Ph.D) Regulations, 2016 notified on 5\nth July, 2016: \n (i) The Indira Gandhi National Open  University (IGNOU), New Delhi,   \n (ii) The Madhya Pradesh Bhoj  (Open) University, Bhopal,  \n (iii) The Pt. Sundarlal (Open) University, Chhattisgarh and  \n (iv) The Uttarakhand (Open)  University, Uttarakhand \n \n   These Universities have also submitted Affida vits to the effect that  they will follow the \nsaid Regulations, 2016 of the UGC strictly without any deviation.  \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |775", "t": "Funds Sanctioned under PRASAD Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.775  \nANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nFUNDS SANCTIONED UNDER PRASAD SCHEME  \n \n775.  SHRI K.C. VENUGOPAL:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the funds sanctioned and utilis ed under PRASAD scheme for the \nintegrated development of pilgrimage destinations in a planned, \nprioritised and sustainable manner to various States including \nKerala;  \n(b)  whether the Government has included Sabarimala and Guruvayur \nunder PRASAD scheme for  the development of these popular pilgrim \ncentres;  \n(c)  if so the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n(d)  the reaction of the Government in this regard?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a) to (d): The details of funds sanctioned and utilised under the PRASAD \nScheme (National Mission on Pilgrimage Rejuvenation And Spiritual \nAugmentation Drive) since its launch  in 2014 -15 to various State  \nGovernments is annexed. \n \nThe Ministry has identifi ed Guruvayur in Kerala as one of the 23 sites \nfor development under PRASAD Scheme in 2016 -17. \n \nThe Ministry of Tourism has also launched Swadesh Darshan scheme \nin 2014 -15 for integrated development of theme based tourist circuits in \nthe country. Under the Scheme the Ministry has sanctioned following two \nprojects for development of Sabarimala in Kerala:  \n \n(i)    ‘Development of Sabarimala -Erumeli-Pampa-Sannidhanam as a \nSpiritual Circuit in  District Pathanamthitta’, in Kerala with \nCentral Financial Assistance of Rs.99.99 crore. \n(ii)   ‘Development of Sree Padmanabha Arnamula -Sabarimala-as a \nSpiritual Circuit’ in Kerala with Cent ral Financial Assistance of \nRs.92.44 crore. \n***********\n\n      ANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART S (a) TO (d) OF L OK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 775 ANNSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \nREGARDING FUNDS SANCTIONED UNDER PRASAD SCHEME.  \n \nState wise details of funds sanctioned under PRASAD Scheme \nduring the year 2014-15, 2015-16 & 2016-17: \n    (Rs. in Crore) \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the project Amt. \nSanctioned \nAmt. \nReleased \n 2014-15   \n BIHAR   \n1. Development of basic facilities at \nVishnupad temple, Gaya, Bihar  \n4.27 2.14 \n UTTAR PRADESH   \n2. Development of Mathura -Vrindavan as \nMega Tourist Circuit (Ph-II) \n14.93 2.99 \n3. Construction of Tourist Facilitation \nCentre at Vrindavan, Distt. Mathura  \n9.36 1.76 \n ODISHA   \n4. Infrastructure Development at Puri, \nShree Jagannath Dham - Ramachandi-\nPrachi River front at Deuli under Mega \nCircuit \n50.00 10.00 \n Total 78.56 16.89 \n 2015-16   \n PUNJAB   \n1. Development of Karuna S agar Valmiki \nSthal at Amritsar \n6.45 3.23 \n RAJASTHAN   \n2. Integrated Development of \nPushkar/Ajmer \n40.44 8.09 \n ASSAM   \n3. Development of Kamakhya Temple and \nPilgrimage Destination in and around \nGuwahati \n33.98 6.80 \n ANDHRA PRADESH   \n4. Development of Am aravati Town, \nGuntur District as a Tourist \nDestination \n28.36 14.18 \n\n BIHAR   \n5. Development at Patna Sahib  41.54 20.77 \n UTTAR PRADESH   \n6. Development of Varanasi 20.40 4.08 \n UTTARAKHAND   \n7. Integrated Development of Kedarnath  34.78 17.39 \n Total 205.95 74.54 \n 2016-17   \n GUJARAT   \n1. Development of Dwarka 26.23 5.25 \n TAMILNADU   \n2. Development of Kanchipuram  16.48 3.30 \n3. Development of Velankanni 5.60 1.12 \n Total 48.31 9.67 \n \n*********** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |776", "t": "Development of Tourism at Mount Abu", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†776 \nANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF TOURISM AT MOUNT ABU  \n \n†776. SHRI DEVJI M. PATEL:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has any proposal to develop world \nfamous Mount Abu of Rajasthan as a tourist/eco friendly circuit \ncentre by providing ropeway upto the Aburda Devi temple, \naquarium, amphitheater, sound and light programmes, zoo, \nparking and projector hall through the use of Corpor ate Social \nResponsibility Fund;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c)  the funds allocated and utilized for the purpose so far?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a) to (c): Development of tourism infrastr ucture is primarily the \nresponsibility of the State Governments and Union Territory \nAdministrations including the State of Rajasthan.  \n \nHowever, the Union Ministry of Tourism provides Central \nFinancial Assistance to them under Swadesh Darshan and PRASAD \nScheme based on project proposals submitted by them subject to \nreceipt of Detailed Project Report (DPR), liquidation of Utilization \nCertificates for the projects sanctioned earlier, availability of funds \nand adherence to relevant scheme guidelines.  \n \nThe Sta te Government of Rajasthan has informed that they \nhave not submitted any such proposal to develop world famous \nMount Abu in Rajasthan. \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |777", "t": "Pollution by Steel Plants", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.777 \nFOR ANSWER ON 21/11/2016 \n \nPOLLUTION BY STEEL PLANTS \n \n777. SHRI CHANDRAKANT KHAIRE: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the environmental norms  are reportedly not being taken into  \nconsideration in the Bokaro Steel Plant; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reaction of the Government thereto; \n \n(c) the number of times warning has  been given and fine slapped by State  \nPollution Control Board against Bokaro  Steel Plant for violation of environmental  \nnorms during the last three years and the current year; and \n \n(d) the corrective steps taken / being  taken by the Government to ensure that all  \nthe steel plants in the country including  the Bokaro Steel Plant strictly adhere to  the \nlaid down environmental norms? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR STEEL         (SHRI VISHNU DEO SAI) \n \n(a)to(c): The environmental norms are being taken into consideration in the Bokaro  \nSteel Plant (BSL). However, during the last three years, the Central Pollution Control \nBoard (CPCB), Ministry of Environment, Forest & Climate Change (MOEF&CC) have \nissued directions to the BSL on 10.06.2013, 10.01.2014 and 08.02.2016 for \ncompliance with environmental norms. \n \n(d) The following Corrective steps have been taken by the Government to ensure \nthat all Steel Plants in the country comply with the environmental norms: \n \n- Old / existing steel plants in the country are being modernised and renovated \nto   adopt environment friendly clean and green technologies. \n- New / green -field steel plants have been /are being set up with state -of –the- \nart   technologies which are capable to meeting environmental norms.   \n- Environmental standards for emissions and effl uent from Iron & Steel Plants \nhave been notified. The standards are amended from time to time. \n- At the instance of MOEF&CC/CPCB, Online Monitoring Systems have been \ninstalled in major integrated iron/steel plants to monitor compliance with air \nemission and effluent discharge norms. \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |778", "t": "Welfare of Workers of Unorganised Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n LOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 778 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nWELFARE OF WORKERS OF UNORGANISED SECTOR \n \n778. SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)the details of health benefit schemes being implemented by \nGovernment for the welfare of workers of unorganized sector; \n(b)whethed the Government has any proposal to include more \ncategories of workers under Employee's State Insurance (ESI) \nscheme and if so, the details thereof; \n(c)the details of compensation awarded by the Government for \nnegligence in treatment of patients in hospitals run by ESIC; and \n(d)the other measures taken by the Government to protect the \ninterests of workers of unorganized sector? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n(a):   The Rashtriya Swasthya Bima Yojana  (RSBY) is a centrally \nsponsored scheme to provide health insurance coverage to Below \nPoverty Line (BPL) families and including other 11 categories of \nUnorganized Workers (MGNREGA Workers, Construction Workers, \nDomestic workers, Sanitation Workers, Mine Wo rkers, licensed \nRailway Porters, Street Vendors, Beedi Workers, Rickshaw Pullers, \nRag Pickers and Auto/Taxi drivers). Each family enrolled in the scheme \nis entitled to hospitalization benefits of upto INR 30,000 per annum \nincluding maternity benefits on a family floater basis ( a unit of five) in \nGovernment empanelled hospitals (includes both private and public). \nPre-existing conditions are covered from day one and there is no age \nlimit. Transportation Cost upto Rs . 100/- per hospitalization subject to \na maximum of Rs. 1000/- per family per year is also provisioned under \nthe scheme. \n \nContd..2/- \n\n::  2  :: \n \n(b) & (c): It has been the constant endeavour of the Government to \nbring more and more unorganised workers under the Employee State \nInsurance Scheme. Recen tly, ESIC has formulated Health Schemes to \ncover Auto Rickshaw Drivers in Delhi and Domestic workers in Delhi \nand Hyderabad on pilot basis.  \n \n(d):  In order to provide social security benefits to the workers in \nthe unorganised sector, the Government has enacted the Unorganised \nWorkers’ Social Security Act, 2008. The 2008 Act stipulates \nformulation of suitable welfare schemes for unorganised workers on \nmatters relating to: (i) life and disability cover, (ii) health and \nmaternity benefits, (iii) old age prote ction and (iv) any other benefit as \nmay be determined by the Central Government through the National \nSocial Security Board. Various Schemes, formulated by the \nGovernment to provide social security cover to the unorganized \nworkers, listed in the Schedule I of the above Act are as under:  \ni. Indira Gandhi National Old Age Pension Scheme. (Ministry of \nRural Development) \nii. National Family Benefit Scheme. (Ministry of Rural Development) \niii. Janani Suraksha Yojana. (Ministry of Health and Family Welfare) \niv. Handloom Weavers’ Comprehensive Welfare Scheme. (Ministry of \nTextiles) \nv. Handicraft Artisans’ Comprehensive Welfare Scheme. (Ministry of \nTextiles) \nvi. Pension to Master Craft Persons. (Ministry of Textiles) \nvii. National Scheme for Welfare of Fishermen an d Training and \nExtension. (Department of Animal Husbandry, Dairying & \nFisheries) \nviii. Aam Admi Bima Yojana. (Department of Financial Services) \nix. Rashtriya Swasthya Bima Yojana. (Ministry of Health and Family \nWelfare) \n              \n            Central Government has also launched the Atal  Pension \nYojana, Pradhan Mantri Jeevan Jyoti Bima Yojana and Pradhan Mantri \nSuraksha Bima Yojana for all citizens especially targeting unorganised \nworkers to provide them comprehensive social security. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |779", "t": "Gas Based Economy", "head": " \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 779 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nGAS BASED ECONOMY  \n \n779.     SHRIMATI JAYSHREEBEN PATEL:                \n \n Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state \n \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkd`frd xSl ea=h \n \n(a)   the concrete steps taken by the Government to make India a gas based economy and \nits  likely impact on petroleum and natural gas production along with its prices; and  \n \n(b)  the amount of expenditure likely to be incurred by the Government thereon? \n \nANSWER \n \nपेट्रोलियम और प्राकृलिक गैस मंत्रािय में राज् य मंत्ररी (स् विंत्र प्राार  \n(श्ररी धमेन्‍द र प्रधान  \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nPETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a): Natural gas is one of the cleanest and most environm ent friendly fuels having \nextremely low Carbon Dioxide emissions compared to other fuels like coal and oil. The \nGovernment has taken the following steps to make India a gas based economy: \ni. Development of Gas Sources either through Domestic gas Exploratio n & Production \nactivities or through building up facilities to import natural gas in the form of LNG, \nii. Development of Gas Pipeline Infrastructure and Secondary distribution network.  \niii. Development of gas consuming markets like Fertilizer, Power, Tran sport and \nIndustries etc.   \n \n Fertilizer and Power Sectors are the major gas consumers in Indian gas market. They \nconsume about 60% of total gas consumption at country level in FY 2015 -16. In order to \ndevelop the gas consuming market, Government has implem ented Fertilizer Gas Pooling \nScheme which has encouraged the utilization of installed Fertilizer Units in the country.  \n \n On the Power Sector, Government has come up with a scheme to make operational \nthe stranded gas based power plants on R -LNG. It has helped in reviving Stranded Power \nplants of around 16,000 MW capacity and saved them from becoming NPA. LNG \nconsumption in Power sector has increased from the level of 3 MMSCMD during April -2015 \n(prior to Pooling) to a maximum level of 11.47 MMSCMD during March-2016.  \n \n\n Further, the Government has taken several steps to enhance domestic  natural gas \nproduction through several policy initiatives such as:  \n \n(i)  Policy to grant relaxation, extension, and clarifications at development and \n production stage for early monetization of hydrocarbon discoveries \n(ii)  Marginal Field Policy- Discovered Small Field Policy \n(iii)  Uniform Licensing Policy-Hydrocarbon Exploration and Licensing Policy \n(iv)  Policy for Grant of Extension to small and medium sized discovered fields \n(v)  Policy for Marketing Freedom for Gas Produced from Deepwater and Ultra \n Deepwater areas etc.; and \n(vi)  Policy on testing requirements for discoveries made under New Exploration and \n Licensing Policy (NELP) Blocks. \n \nTo incentivize gas production from difficult areas, Government has granted marketing, \nincluding pricing, freedom for the gas produced from difficult areas. Marketing freedom has \nalso been provided under Discovered Small Field bidding round as well as under  \nHydrocarbon Exploration and Licensing Policy (HELP) under which acreages will be \nprovided in future.  \n \nThe implementation of these policy initiatives and other reform initiatives is expected to \nenhance domestic natural gas production from the fields.   \n \n(b):  The Government normally does not provide any financial support for laying pipeline \ninfrastructure. However, Government has taken a decision to provide a capital grant of Rs. 5176 \ncrore (i.e. 40% of the estimated capital cost of Rs.12,940 Crore) to GAI L for development of a \n2539 Km long Jagdishpur -Haldia/Bokaro-Dhamra Gas Pipeline (JHBDPL) project to connect \nEastern part of the country with National Gas Grid. \n \n During the financial year 2015 -2016, the CPSEs have incurred an approximate amount of \nRs. 2962 crore on natural gas and other petroleum products pipeline infrastructure development. \n \n \n \n \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |78", "t": "Scheme for the development", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING \n \nLOK  SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 78  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 16.11.2016 \n \n \nSCHEME FOR THE DEVELOPMENT \n \n \n78. SHRI BHAIRON PRASAD MISHRA \n \nWill the MINISTER of PLANNING be pleased to state: \n \n \n(a) Whether Government propose to formulate any special schemes for the development of \ndistricts under the Bundelkhand backward area in the coming five year plan; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) if not, whether Government propose to consider any such scheme; and \n(d) if so, the details there of? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT AND  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING & URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \n \n(RAO INDERJIT SINGH) \n \n \n(a & b) No Madam, \n \n(c & d) Does not arise. \n \n \n----------  \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|10|UNSTARRED |780", "t": "Scholarship provided by CCRT", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 780\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016\n \n \nSCHOLARSHIP PROVIDED BY CCRT\n \n \n \n780. SHRI B. SRIRAMULU:\n \n \nWill the Minister of \nCULTURE\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe amount given as scholarships to\n \nstudents by the Centre for Cultural Resources \nand Training (CCRT);\n \n \n(b)\n \nthe number of recipients of the said scholarship during each\n \nof the last two years \nand the \ncurrent year, State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(c)\n \nwhether there is any proposal to increase the amount and the\n \nnumber of \nscholarships awarded; and\n \n \n(d)\n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR CULTURE & TOURISM \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \nANSWER\n \n(a)\n \nUnder the Scheme ‘Cultural Talent Search Scholarship Scheme (CTSSS)’, an amount \nof Rs.3600/\n-\n \nper year is g\niven to each scholarship holder of age group of 10 to 14 \nyears, in addition to reimbursement of the actual tuition fee paid to the \nGuru/Teacher, up to a ceiling of Rs.9000/\n-\n \nper year.\n \nIn addition to above CTSS Scheme, the CCRT has recently been entrusted p\nartly \nwith the task of implementing scheme for ‘Award of Scholarship to Young Artists \n(SYA)’ under which scholarships are awarded to students in the age group of 18 to \n25 years and Rs. 5000/\n-\n \nper month are reimbursed for the advance training in \nvarious cul\ntural art forms. \n \n \n(b)\n \nState\n-\nwise break\n-\nup of recipients of the scholarship during the last two years and \nthe current year is given at Annexure. \n \n(c)\n \nNo, Madam.\n \n(d)\n \nDoes not arise. \n \n***\n \n\n \nAnnexure\n \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (b) of the \n \nLok Sabh\na Unstarred Question No. 780 for answer on 21.11.2016\n \n \nState wise break\n-\nup of the Scholarships awarded under Cultural Talent Search \nScholarship Scheme for the year 2014\n-\n15, 2015\n-\n16 and 2016\n-\n17\n \n \nS.No.\n \nSTATE/UTs\n \nYEAR\n \n2014\n-\n2015\n \nYEAR \n2015\n-\n16\n \nYEAR \n2016\n-\n17\n \n1.\n \nA\nndhra Pradesh\n \n35\n \n16\n \n10\n \n2.\n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n02\n \n-\n \n-\n \n3.\n \nAssam\n \n84\n \n86\n \n74\n \n4.\n \nBihar\n \n08\n \n11\n \n08\n \n5.\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n09\n \n11\n \n02\n \n6.\n \nGoa\n \n05\n \n-\n \n32\n \n7.\n \nGujarat\n \n10\n \n10\n \n-\n \n8.\n \nHaryana\n \n05\n \n05\n \n09\n \n9.\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n01\n \n01\n \n06\n \n10.\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n10\n \n04\n \n-\n \n11.\n \nJharkhand\n \n09\n \n11\n \n-\n \n12.\n \nKarnataka\n \n36\n \n47\n \n05\n \n13.\n \nKerala\n \n31\n \n44\n \n26\n \n14.\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n21\n \n17\n \n47\n \n15.\n \nMaharashtra\n \n46\n \n46\n \n18\n \n16.\n \nManipur\n \n14\n \n20\n \n33\n \n17.\n \nMeghalaya\n \n08\n \n01\n \n13\n \n18.\n \nMizoram\n \n00\n \n00\n \n-\n \n19.\n \nNagaland\n \n13\n \n00\n \n-\n \n20.\n \nOdisha\n \n64\n \n53\n \n-\n \n21.\n \nPunjab\n \n06\n \n06\n \n50\n \n22.\n \nRajasthan\n \n07\n \n09\n \n11\n \n23\n.\n \nSikkim\n \n02\n \n00\n \n05\n \n24.\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n36\n \n19\n \n16\n \n25.\n \nTelangana\n \n--\n \n26\n \n-\n \n26.\n \nTripura\n \n37\n \n41\n \n22\n \n27.\n \nUttarakhand\n \n03\n \n01\n \n28\n \n28.\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n14\n \n20\n \n45\n \n29.\n \nWest Bengal\n \n47\n \n56\n \n15\n \n30.\n \nA & N\n \nIslands\n \n03\n \n02\n \n02\n \n31.\n \nChandigarh\n \n01\n \n01\n \n47\n \n32.\n \nDelhi\n \n33\n \n48\n \n-\n \n33.\n \nDadar & Nag\nar Haveli\n \n00\n \n00\n \n02\n \n34.\n \nDaman & Diu